4:      \"Resolves to Wyoming's ex parte renewal share for the August 2026 reporting period, computed from the original (O) submission row in CMS dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c when CMS first publishes it (expected roughly three to four months after the period). Numerator and denominator as published; share computed to one decimal.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:5239:    note: \"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Wyoming Medicaid ex parte renewal share, August 2026.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6325:      \"Resolves to California's procedural disenrollments divided by total disenrollments at renewal for the August 2026 reporting period (original submission row, CMS dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c), conditional on the event 'California ex parte renewals divided by total completed renewals is at least 80.0 percent in the same row.' If the conditioning event does not occur, the cell is marked unresolved.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6330:    note: \"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for California procedural share | ex parte at or above 80%.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:8767:        \"state renewal operations and reporting methodology remain the main downside risk\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:9234:          text: \"Counter-consideration and scenarios: downside outside the interval would require April updated enrollment more than about 1.59 million below March, likely from broad state renewal drops or reporting cleanups. Upside outside the interval would require April updated enrollment more than about 1.31 million above March, likely from unusually large retroactive enrollment, state resubmissions, or a reporting-break rebound. The central case is near-flat to mildly down from March.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:9263:        \"state renewal operations remain the main downside channel\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:9693:          text: \"Policy and one-off mechanisms: upside would come from late state processing stabilization, continuous-eligibility effects, or stronger CHIP retention; downside would come from renewal backlogs, eligibility redeterminations, and state reporting corrections hitting the preliminary count.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:9705:          text: \"Outside-the-interval scenarios: below 77,250,000 would likely require unusually broad renewal-related terminations or a reporting break; above 78,900,000 would likely require a material state resubmission effect or a broad March enrollment rebound.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-coverage.ts:51:      \"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.ca.aug_2026\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-coverage.ts:54:      \"Renewed-total counts are denominator inputs for the California ex parte renewal share target.\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-coverage.ts:60:      \"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.ca.aug_2026\",\nsite/src/data/ledger-coverage.ts:63:      \"Ex parte renewal counts are numerator inputs for the California ex parte renewal share target.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:73:      \"What share of California Medicaid beneficiaries disenrolled at renewal in the August 2026 reporting period will be disenrolled for procedural reasons, per the CMS eligibility processing dataset?\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:83:      \"Resolves to beneficiaries disenrolled for procedural reasons divided by total beneficiaries disenrolled at renewal, California, August 2026 reporting period, original (O) submission row in CMS dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c, computed when CMS first publishes the row (expected roughly three to four months after the period).\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:92:      \"Ex parte renewal expansion via CalSAWS data matching\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:94:      \"Notice redesign and renewal outreach efforts\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:95:      \"Month-to-month renewal cohort mix\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:122:        text: \"The mechanism is compositional: as ex parte automation renews the determinably eligible without paperwork, the people who still lose coverage are increasingly those who missed a form. That pushes the share toward an asymptote in the mid-90s. The downside tail covers notice reforms or an eligibility-policy change that grows the determined-ineligible denominator.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2020:    title: \"Alaska Medicaid ex parte renewal share, August 2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2021:    question: \"What share of completed Medicaid renewals in Alaska will be processed on an ex parte basis in the August 2026 reporting period, per the CMS eligibility processing dataset?\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2029:    resolutionRule: \"Resolves to Alaska's ex parte renewal share for the August 2026 reporting period, computed from the original (O) submission row in CMS dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c when CMS first publishes it (expected roughly three to four months after the period). Numerator and denominator as published; share computed to one decimal.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2030:    dataPointId: \"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.ak.aug_2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2032:    drivers: [\"Data-matching coverage across wage, SNAP, and vital records sources\", \"Eligibility system modernization and vendor release schedules\", \"Renewal cohort composition month to month\", \"CMS renewal-policy requirements and state compliance plans\"],\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2033:    reasoning: [{ kind: \"heading\", text: \"The series\" }, { kind: \"text\", text: \"Ex parte share measures how much of the renewal burden the state carries instead of the beneficiary. It trends with data-matching infrastructure, which improves in vendor-release steps rather than smoothly.\" }, { kind: \"tool\", tool: \"cms.lookup\", call: \"cms.lookup({ state: \\\"AK\\\", dataset: \\\"eligibility_processing\\\", series: \\\"ex_parte_renewal_share\\\", latest: true })\", result: \"{ period: \\\"2026-02\\\", value: 56.2 }\" }, { kind: \"math\", text: \"Latest 56.2 (02/26); mean monthly change over trailing window -0.93pp; damped six-month projection = 53.4.\" }, { kind: \"heading\", text: \"Forecast\" }, { kind: \"text\", text: \"Half-weight trend continuation with volatility-scaled uncertainty; renewal cohorts differ month to month, so the interval is wider than the point trend alone would suggest.\" }, { kind: \"forecast\", point: 53.4, ciLow: 40.5, ciHigh: 66.2 }],\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2038:    title: \"Alabama Medicaid ex parte renewal share, August 2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2039:    question: \"What share of completed Medicaid renewals in Alabama will be processed on an ex parte basis in the August 2026 reporting period, per the CMS eligibility processing dataset?\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2047:    resolutionRule: \"Resolves to Alabama's ex parte renewal share for the August 2026 reporting period, computed from the original (O) submission row in CMS dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c when CMS first publishes it (expected roughly three to four months after the period). Numerator and denominator as published; share computed to one decimal.\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2048:    dataPointId: \"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.al.aug_2026\",\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:2050:    drivers: [\"Data-matching coverage across wage, SNAP, and vital records sources\", \"Eligibi