SD billion, refunds were 22 USD billion, fiscal-year net tariff revenue through May was 189 USD billion versus 81 USD billion a year earlier, and the calendar-adjusted deficit was 2 percent or 24 USD billion below the prior year.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13290:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"I start with a recent-June base of about (27 + 66) / 2 = 46.5 billion, add roughly 20 billion for higher interest and entitlement outlays, add 10 billion for tariff-refund and policy noise, and round to a 72 billion point. The 80% interval is set wide at -35 to 210 billion to cover a possible June surplus from strong receipts and a 2023-like high-deficit outlier if payment timing or refunds raise outlays.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13294:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: elevated tariff receipts and June tax collections could produce a small surplus, especially if refunds are not concentrated in June, which is why the lower bound is negative despite the point being a deficit.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13468:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13472:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Start from a recent-June base rate around 90 billion. Add roughly 55 billion for normal June benefit and Medicare/HHS payments not shifted into May, add about 25 billion for higher interest and entitlement growth, and subtract about 15 billion for still-elevated customs/tariff receipts net of refunds: 90 + 55 + 25 - 15 = 155 billion. Set an 80% interval of 20 to 310 billion to cover tax-payment and refund volatility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/agent-runs.ts:517:    seriesId: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"irs.filing_season.total_refunds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/agent-runs.ts:529:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"IRS cumulative refunds connect filing behavior, withholding, refundable credits, processing pace, and tax-year liability into a near-term administrative target.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/agent-runs.ts:546:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"{ may_8_2026_refunds_billions: 324.757, average_refund: 3276, historical_may_to_october_addition_billions: 41.8 }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/agent-runs.ts:550:        slug: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/agent-runs.ts:551:        title: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"IRS cumulative refunds, October 2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/2026-06-22/digest.json:986:   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecastSlug\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/2026-06-22/digest.json:2416:   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecastSlug\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/2026-06-23/digest.json:986:   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecastSlug\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/2026-06-23/digest.json:2416:   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecastSlug\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/2026-06-12/digest.json:876:   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecastSlug\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"irs-total-refunds-october-2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/2026-06-12/digest.json:1366:   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecastSlug\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/2026-06-15/digest.json:876:   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecastSlug\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"irs-total-r