\\\\\\\\\\\\"resolutionSource\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Official U.S. Treasury Monthly Treasury Statement\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/summary-of-receipts-outlays-and-the-deficit-surplus-of-the-u-s-government\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resolutionRule\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolve to the first Monthly Treasury Statement print for June 2026, Table 1 total deficit/surplus for FY 2026 June. Convert the published $ millions to $ billions and record deficits as positive and surpluses as negative. Do not revise for later restatements or annual combined statements.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"dataPointId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"treasury.mts.table1.total_deficit_surplus.2026-06.first_print\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"historicalContext\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2025-06 first-print deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":-27.01},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2024-06 first-print deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":65.965},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2023-06 first-print deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":227.768},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2022-06 first-print deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":88.842},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-Q2 Treasury borrowing estimate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":189}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"drivers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June corporate tax receipts partially offset outlays\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June 1, 2026 falls on a business day, so fewer payments should shift into May than in 2024 or 2025\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"higher net interest and entitlement outlays than recent years\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tariff-refund shock reduces a revenue tailwind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"FYTD deficit was reported slightly below the prior year through May after calendar adjustment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sourceContext\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/accounting/monthly-treasury-statement/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/accounting/monthly-treasury-statement/previous\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0625.pdf\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0624.pdf\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0622.pdf\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/summary-of-receipts-outlays-and-the-deficit-surplus-of-the-u-s-government\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"runAt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-06-21T15:08:15Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reasoning\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Forecast for June 2026 Monthly Treasury Statement deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolver: use the first official U.S. Treasury Monthly Treasury Statement for June 2026, Table 1 total deficit/surplus, converted from $ millions to $ billions, with deficits positive and surpluses negative.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Opened the Bureau of the Fiscal Service Monthly Treasury Statement landing page and current-issue links.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The official MTS page says the statement covers receipts, outlays, surplus or deficit, and means of financing; it also says data moved to FiscalData on 2025-11-25 and the MTS page was last updated 2026-01-15.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Opened the June 2025 official MTS PDF and read Table 1 plus highlights.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June 2025 receipts were 526.445 billion, outlays were 499.435 billion, and the deficit/surplus was -27.010 billion; FYTD receipts were 4008.146 billion, FYTD outlays were 5345.518 billion, and FYTD deficit was 1337.372 billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Opened the June 2024 official MTS PDF and read Table 1.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June 2024 receipts were 466.255 billion, outlays were 532.220 billion, and the deficit was 65.965 billion; June 2023 receipts were 418.317 billion, outlays were 646.085 billion, and the deficit was 227.768 billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Opened the June 2022 official MTS PDF and read Table 1 and the summary chart.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June 2022 receipts were 460.761 billion, outlays were 549.603 billion, and the deficit was 88.842 billion; FYTD deficit through June 2022 was 515.067 billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"public.lookup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Checked public reporting on Treasury's May 2026 fiscal context and quarterly borrowing estimates.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Reports cited Treasury figures that May 2026 net tariff revenue was 0 billion after 22 billion collected and 22 billion refunded, FYTD net tariff revenue was 189 billion versus 81 billion a year earlier, adjusted FYTD deficit was 2 percent or 24 billion below the prior year, and Treasury's April-June 2026 borrowing estimate was 189 billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Base-rate: recent June deficits are volatile because corporate tax collections and payment-date shifts matter. The 2022-2025 June outcomes were 88.842, 227.768, 65.965, and -27.010 billion, giving a simple four-year average near 88.9 billion and a three-year average near 88.2 billion, but the 2024 and 2025 June figures were held down by June 1 weekend payment acceleration into May.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Start from a recent-June base rate around 90 billion. Add roughly 55 billion for normal June benefit and Medicare/HHS payments not shifted into May, add about 25 billion for higher interest and entitlement growth, and subtract about 15 billion for still-elevated customs/tariff receipts net of refunds: 90 + 55 + 25 - 15 = 155 billion. Set an 80% interval of 20 to 310 billion to cover tax-payment and refund volatility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":155,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":20,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":310}]}\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:101:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:105:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Start from a recent-June base rate around 90 billion. Add roughly 55 billion for normal June benefit and Medicare/HHS payments not shifted into May, add about 25 billion for higher interest and entitlement growth, and subtract about 15 billion for still-elevated customs/tariff receipts net of refunds: 90 + 55 + 25 - 15 = 155 billion. Set an 80% interval of 20 to 310 billion to cover tax-payment and refund volatility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:101:    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:105:    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Start from a recent-June base rate around 90 billion. Add roughly 55 billion for normal June benefit and Medicare/HHS payments not shifted into May, add about 25 billion for higher interest and entitlement growth, and subtract about 15 billion for still-elevated customs/tariff receipts net of refunds: 90 + 55 + 25 - 15 = 155 billion. Set an 80% interval of 20 to 310 billion to cover tax-payment and refund volatility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-57z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:101:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\": 0, \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-17-54z-treasury-mts-individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026-fy2026/draft_codex_events.jsonl:53:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"item_27\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rg -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tariff revenue|customs duties|individual income tax|refund|receipts were|outlays were|FYTD receipts|fiscal-year\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" records/thesis-analyst site/src/data -g '*.json' -g '*.ts' -g '*.txt' | head -200\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"site/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:593:          '{ point: 13.0, ci80: [12.1, 14.1], drivers: [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ctc_refundability\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"earnings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"housing_costs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"] }',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:643:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The official poverty measure excludes taxes, refundable credits, and noncash benefits, so it is a cleaner near-term read on cash-income and labor-market strength than SPM.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1049:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The SPM child poverty rate for 2027 is dominated by the configuration of refundable credits under whatever tax regime is in force, plus the labor market. The CTC is the single largest policy lever \\\\\\\\u2014 it directly raised the CTC sensitivity of SPM child poverty by roughly 4 percentage points during the 2021 expansion. The 2027 reading depends critically on how TCJA-extension legislation resolves.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1062:          '{ point: 11.4, ci80: [10.5, 12.4], drivers: [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"CTC remains $2,000 / child\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1,700 refundable portion preserved\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EITC parameters unchanged\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"] }',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1068:          '{ point: 12.2, ci80: [11.3, 13.1], drivers: [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"CTC at $1,500 / child\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"refundability cap retained\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"EITC reverts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"] }',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1084:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"House passed a TCJA extension package in March. Senate markup is expected Q3. Conditional on the Senate moving a vehicle, full extension is the modal outcome but partial scaling (especially on CTC refundability) is a live possibility. Weighting:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1550:    title: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Federal individual income tax revenue, FY2027\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1552:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"What will total federal individual income tax revenue be for fiscal year 2027 in nominal dollars, as published in the Monthly Treasury Statement?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1562:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total individual income tax receipts for FY2027 as reported in the Monthly Treasury Statement covering September 2027, in billions of nominal dollars.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1840:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Maximum is the headline parameter \\\\\\\\u2014 refundability and phase-in are separate forecast cells. Current law: $2,000/child through TY2025, reverting to $1,000/child in TY2026 absent legislation. Any TCJA extension would lock at $2,000 (= $167/mo) or higher.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1883:    title: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Cost of a $3,000 fully refundable CTC, TY2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1885:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"What will official scorers estimate as the federal budget cost of a $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit for tax year 2026, relative to current law?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1895:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the first official JCT or CBO estimate, in billions of nominal dollars, for the tax-year 2026 federal budget effect of a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit scored against current law.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1896:    policyParameter: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gov.irs.credits.ctc.refundable.fully_refundable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1957:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal outlays/refundable-credit cost attributable to the Child Tax Credit for tax year 2026, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS/Treasury table that separately identifies the CTC.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1975:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This is the baseline CTC outlay cell the expansion-cost forecast compares against. It needs a tax-year measure, not a fiscal-year cash-flow proxy, because official outlay timing can shift refunds across fiscal years.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1983:          '{ point: 58.8, ci80: [54.0, 64.5], drivers: [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"eligible children\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"refundability cap\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"phase-in earnings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"] }',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2016:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal outlays/refundable-credit cost attributable to the Earned Income Tax Credit for tax year 2026, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS/Treasury table that separately identifies the EITC.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2046:        call: 'irs.lookup({ table: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"soi_historical_refundable_credits\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", credit: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"eitc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", years: [2021, 2024] })',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2063:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"If the SALT deduction cap is set at $40,000 for joint filers in tax year 2027, what will the federal individual income tax revenue loss be relative to a $10,000 cap?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2251:    title: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Top individual income tax rate, TY2027\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2253:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"What will the top federal individual income tax marginal rate be for tax year 2027?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2262:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the highest statutory federal individual income tax marginal rate applicable for TY2027, excluding surtaxes such as NIIT or Medicare taxes.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2464:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal SNAP benefit outlays for FY2027, excluding administrative costs, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official USDA FNS or Treasury fiscal-year table that identifies SNAP benefits.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2521:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal outlays for refundable premium tax credits under the Affordable Care Act for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or CBO table that separately identifies the credit.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2694:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal unemployment insurance benefit outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or Department of Labor fiscal-year table that reports UI benefits.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2751:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal SSI benefit payments for FY2027, excluding state supplements, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official SSA or Treasury fiscal-year table that reports SSI federal payments.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2807:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal Medicaid outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or CMS fiscal-year table that reports federal Medicaid payments.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:2899:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The interval keeps a small upside tail for outreach or refundability changes that pull non-filers into the tax system.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3033:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to OASDI benefit payments for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official SSA or Treasury fiscal-year table that reports Old-Age, Survivors, and Disability Insurance benefits.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3076:    slug: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aotc-refundable-outlays-ty2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3080:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"What will federal refundable American Opportunity Tax Credit outlays be for tax year 2026, in nominal dollars?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3090:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to refundable American Opportunity Tax Credit outlays for TY2026, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS SOI or Treasury table that separately identifies the refundable AOTC amount.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3092:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gov.irs.credits.education.american_opportunity.refundable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3106:      { kind: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Identifying the refundable-credit target\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" },\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3114:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ policy: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"current_law\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", year: 2026, output: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"refundable_aotc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", unit: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"billions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" })',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3121:        call: 'irs.lookup({ table: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"soi_refundable_education_credits\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", credit: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aotc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", years: [2019, 2025] })',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3315:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal Medicare benefit outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury, CMS, or CBO fiscal-year table that reports Medicare benefit payments.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3372:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal TANF outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official HHS ACF or Treasury table that reports federal TANF fiscal-year spending.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3530:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"How many individual income tax returns will claim the Earned Income Tax Credit for tax year 2027?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3539:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the number of TY2027 individual income tax returns claiming the Earned Income Tax Credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2027 individual returns.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:3814:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the fiscal-year average number of families receiving TANF or Separate State Program Maintenance-of-Effort cash assistance in FY2027, in millions, using the first ACF TANF caseload table covering all FY2027 months.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4251:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal School Breakfast Program cost for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first USDA FNS child nutrition program table that reports fiscal-year federal cost.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4361:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to Federal Pell Grant program outlays or obligations for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Department of Education Federal Student Aid annual report, budget justification, or Pell Grant program data table that reports fiscal-year spending.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4416:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total federal WIC program cost for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first USDA FNS WIC participation and cost table that reports fiscal-year total cost.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4572:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"How many individual income tax returns will claim the federal child and dependent care credit for tax year 2026?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4581:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the federal child and dependent care credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income individual income tax table that reports this credit.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4599:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The child and dependent care credit is a direct PolicyEngine-relevant tax unit output. Claimant returns are useful because the nonrefundable credit can have stable claims even when revenue cost changes with income and tax liability.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4627:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"How many individual income tax returns will claim the federal premium tax credit for tax year 2026?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4636:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the premium tax credit or reconciling advance premium tax credits on Form 8962, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2026 individual returns.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4682:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"How many individual income tax returns will claim the refundable Additional Child Tax Credit for tax year 2026?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4691:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the refundable Additional Child Tax Credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2026 individual returns.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4700:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"CTC refundability formula\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4709:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ACTC claimant returns isolate the refundable part of the CTC. That is the channel that matters most for child poverty and for whether a CTC expansion reaches low-income families.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4722:          '{ point: 16.1, ci80: [13.6, 19.2], drivers: [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"phase_in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"refundability_cap\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"eligible_children\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"] }',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4737:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"How much will individual income tax filers deduct for charitable contributions on itemized returns for tax year 2026?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4746:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total charitable contributions deducted on TY2026 itemized individual income tax returns, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income Schedule A or itemized-deductions table covering TY2026.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4897:    slug: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ctc-refundable-amount-ty2027\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4901:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"What will the maximum refundable portion of the federal Child Tax Credit be per qualifying child in tax year 2027?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4910:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the maximum refundable federal Child Tax Credit amount per qualifying child for TY2027, using IRC section 24 and the first IRS Form 1040 instructions or inflation-adjusted tax parameter release that identifies the refundable amount.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4911:    policyParameter: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"irc.24.refundable_amount_per_child.2027\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4928:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The maximum refundable CTC amount is the policy parameter that drives outlays and child-poverty effects for low-income families. It is distinct from the headline maximum credit because families with low earnings can be limited by the refundable cap and phase-in.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4934:        call: 'axiom.query({ statute: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"IRC.24\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", parameter: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"refundable_amount_per_child\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", year: 2027, branch: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"post_sunset_current_law\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" })',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4936:          '{ value: 1000, note: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"credit reverts and refundable structure narrows under scheduled law\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" }',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4941:        call: 'axiom.query({ statute: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"IRC.24\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", parameter: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"refundable_amount_per_child\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", year: 2027, branch: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tcja_extended_indexed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" })',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4943:          '{ value: 1800, note: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"current refundable amount retained and indexed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" }',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4947:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ reform: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ctc_refundable_amount_1800\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", year: 2027, output: [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ctc_outlays\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"spm_child_poverty_rate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"], unit: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"billions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" })',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4949:          '{ outlays: 64.0, child_poverty_rate: 11.6, sensitivity: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"about $5B per additional $100 refundable cap near the phase-in margin\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" }',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4954:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"P(indexed current cap) = 0.55; P($1,000 reversion) = 0.20; P(larger refundable expansion) = 0.20; P(other) = 0.05.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:4958:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The modal outcome is retaining the current refundable design with indexation. The lower tail remains real because the scheduled-law branch is a discrete cliff; the upper tail reflects a child-credit compromise that expands refundability without restoring the full 2021 policy.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5158:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Conditional on a full TCJA extension package (CTC at $2,000, current refundability, current EITC) being enacted by June 30, 2026, what will the SPM child poverty rate be in 2028?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5187:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Conditional resolution: only meaningful if a TCJA-extension package matching the House framework is enacted by June 30, 2026. Under that conditioning event, the CTC ($2,000/child, $1,700 refundable) and EITC are stable through 2028. Question is the residual SPM child-poverty path under stable policy.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5220:    slug: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"child-poverty-2026-given-ctc-3000-refundable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5222:    title: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Child poverty 2026 | $3,000 fully refundable CTC\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5224:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Conditional on a $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for tax year 2026, what will the Supplemental Poverty Measure child poverty rate be for calendar year 2026?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5233:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the official Census SPM child poverty rate for calendar year 2026, conditional on a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable CTC being in effect for TY2026. If the policy is not in effect, the forecast cell is marked unresolved.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5235:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit in effect for tax year 2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5254:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This cell asks what the observed SPM child poverty rate would be if the $3,000 fully refundable CTC policy is actually in force for TY2026. Policy uncertainty is removed; macro and measurement uncertainty remain.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5259:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ reform: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ctc_3000_fully_refundable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", year: 2026, output: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"spm_child_poverty_rate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", map_to: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"person\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" })',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5283:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Federal individual income tax revenue FY2028 | SALT cap fully repealed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5285:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Conditional on the SALT deduction cap being repealed in full (no cap) for tax years 2026 and later, what will federal individual income tax revenue be in FY2028 (in billions of nominal dollars)?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5295:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total individual income tax receipts for FY2028 as reported in the Monthly Treasury Statement covering September 2028, conditional on the SALT deduction cap under IRC \\\\\\\\u00a7164(b)(6) being fully eliminated for TY2026 and later. If the cap is not fully eliminated, the forecast cell is marked unresolved.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:130:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total individual income tax receipts for FY2027 as reported in the Monthly Treasury Statement covering September 2027, in billions of nominal dollars.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:134:    note: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Federal individual income tax revenue, FY2027.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:194:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal SNAP benefit outlays for FY2027, excluding administrative costs, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official USDA FNS or Treasury fiscal-year table that identifies SNAP benefits.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:210:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal outlays for refundable premium tax credits under the Affordable Care Act for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or CBO table that separately identifies the credit.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:258:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal unemployment insurance benefit outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or Department of Labor fiscal-year table that reports UI benefits.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:275:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal SSI benefit payments for FY2027, excluding state supplements, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official SSA or Treasury fiscal-year table that reports SSI federal payments.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:291:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal Medicaid outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury or CMS fiscal-year table that reports federal Medicaid payments.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:357:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to OASDI benefit payments for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official SSA or Treasury fiscal-year table that reports Old-Age, Survivors, and Disability Insurance benefits.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:422:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal Medicare benefit outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Treasury, CMS, or CBO fiscal-year table that reports Medicare benefit payments.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:439:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal TANF outlays for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official HHS ACF or Treasury table that reports federal TANF fiscal-year spending.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:487:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the number of TY2027 individual income tax returns claiming the Earned Income Tax Credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2027 individual returns.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:567:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the fiscal-year average number of families receiving TANF or Separate State Program Maintenance-of-Effort cash assistance in FY2027, in millions, using the first ACF TANF caseload table covering all FY2027 months.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:695:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to federal School Breakfast Program cost for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first USDA FNS child nutrition program table that reports fiscal-year federal cost.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:728:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to Federal Pell Grant program outlays or obligations for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official Department of Education Federal Student Aid annual report, budget justification, or Pell Grant program data table that reports fiscal-year spending.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:744:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total federal WIC program cost for FY2027, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first USDA FNS WIC participation and cost table that reports fiscal-year total cost.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:792:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the federal child and dependent care credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income individual income tax table that reports this credit.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:808:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the premium tax credit or reconciling advance premium tax credits on Form 8962, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2026 individual returns.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:824:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the number of TY2026 individual income tax returns claiming the refundable Additional Child Tax Credit, in millions, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income table covering TY2026 individual returns.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:840:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total charitable contributions deducted on TY2026 itemized individual income tax returns, in billions of nominal dollars, using the first official IRS Statistics of Income Schedule A or itemized-deductions table covering TY2026.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:1202:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"irs.filing_season.total_amount_refunded.october_2026.first_print\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:1204:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"obs.irs.filing_season.total_amount_refunded.october_2026.first_print\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:1211:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to IRS Filing Season Statistics total amount refunded for current-year individual returns in the first October 2026 snapshot. If IRS publishes multiple October snapshots, the earliest October table governs.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:1215:    note: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for IRS cumulative refunds, October 2026.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2300:    dataPointId: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"treasury.mts.individual_income_tax_refunds.fy2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2301:    observationId: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"obs.treasury.mts.individual_income_tax_refunds.fy2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2309:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total individual income tax refunds for fiscal year 2026 in the final Monthly Treasury Statement for September 2026. If Treasury revises the table after the initial final MTS release, the first final fiscal-year table governs.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2313:    note: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Individual income tax refunds, FY2026.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2324:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"IRS Statistics of Income, Affordable Care Act individual income tax items\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2326:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the IRS SOI tax-year 2025 estimate for net premium tax credit reconciliation on individual income tax returns, defined as additional net PTC claimed minus excess advance PTC repaid. If IRS publishes separate line-item and ACA-statistics estimates, the ACA-statistics table governs.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2330:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"IRS Statistics of Income, Affordable Care Act individual income tax items\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2417:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the final U.S. infant mortality rate for calendar year 2026, in infant deaths per 1,000 live births, from CDC/NCHS NVSS linked birth/infant death records, conditional on no materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for TY2026. If such a policy is in effect, this baseline scenario is marked unresolved.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2428:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cdc.nchs.nvss.infant_mortality_rate.2026.ctc_3000_refundable.final\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2430:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"obs.cdc.nchs.nvss.infant_mortality_rate.2026.ctc_3000_refundable.final\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2439:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the final U.S. infant mortality rate for calendar year 2026, in infant deaths per 1,000 live births, from CDC/NCHS NVSS linked birth/infant death records, conditional on a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for TY2026. If the policy is not in effect, this scenario is marked unresolved.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:2445:    note: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Infant mortality 2026 | $3,000 fully refundable CTC.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6721:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the official Census SPM child poverty rate for calendar year 2026, conditional on a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable CTC being in effect for TY2026. If the policy is not in effect, the forecast cell is marked unresolved.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6725:    note: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Child poverty 2026 | $3,000 fully refundable CTC.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6738:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total individual income tax receipts for FY2028 as reported in the Monthly Treasury Statement covering September 2028, conditional on the SALT deduction cap under IRC \\\\\\\\u00a7164(b)(6) being fully eliminated for TY2026 and later. If the cap is not fully eliminated, the forecast cell is marked unresolved.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6742:    note: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Backfilled target registration from the forecast catalog for Federal individual income tax revenue FY2028 | SALT cap fully repealed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2155:  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"child-poverty-2026-given-ctc-3000-refundable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2158:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"child-poverty-2026-given-ctc-3000-refundable-thesis-analyst-fast-2026-06-27t14-21-25z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2168:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"A fully refundable $3,000 CTC directly raises SPM resources through refundable tax credits\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2169:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2021 shows large child-poverty sensitivity to refundable credits, but also included stimulus and larger young-child credits\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2588:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Forecast for 2026 child SPM poverty conditional on a refundable $3,000 CTC\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2592:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau first print for the Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate among people under age 18 for calendar year 2026. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources include taxes, refundable credits, transfers and noncash benefits, and subtract medical, work, and child-care expenses while adjusting thresholds for housing costs.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2599:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched 2021 child SPM poverty rate 5.2 percent, 2020 child SPM 9.7 percent, refundable tax credits moving 9.6 million people out of SPM poverty, and stimulus payments moving 8.9 million people out.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2606:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched 2022 child SPM poverty rate 12.4 percent, up from 5.2 percent in 2021; overall SPM rate 12.4 percent; refundable tax credits moved 6.4 million people out of SPM poverty, down from 9.6 million in 2021.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2631:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Base-rate/reference class: without the 2021 expanded credit and stimulus environment, recent child SPM poverty sits around 12.4 to 13.7 percent. I use 13.4 percent as the current-policy outside-view anchor before adding the conditional $3,000 fully refundable CTC.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2635:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Time-series prior: I do not extrapolate a simple linear trend because the child SPM series has large policy discontinuities from pandemic stimulus, expanded refundable credits, and their expiration; a persistence prior around the recent non-expanded-CTC level is more defensible.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2639:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Policy mechanism: a fully refundable $3,000 CTC would count in SPM tax-credit resources for eligible families and would matter most for children in low-earnings households who receive little or no current-law nonrefundable credit. It should not mechanically recreate the 2021 5.2 percent rate because the 2021 outcome also reflected larger under-age-6 credits, stimulus payments, and pandemic-era safety-net conditions.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2643:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Anchor 13.4 percent. Subtract 4.7 percentage points for the $3,000 fully refundable CTC, smaller than the 7.2 point 2021-to-2022 child SPM reversal because this condition excludes stimulus and the $3,600 young-child amount. Point = 13.4 - 4.7 = 8.7. For the 80% interval, combine about 1.3 points of recent annual child-SPM movement, roughly 1.5-2.0 points of policy-effect uncertainty, and macro/SPM modeling risk, giving an asymmetric judgmental interval of 5.7 to 12.0.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2647:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: an outcome near 5.7 percent or lower could happen if wage growth is broad, housing and food burdens ease, and refundable-credit take-up is near-complete. An outcome near 12.0 percent or higher could happen if unemployment rises, rent or medical out-of-pocket costs lift SPM needs, or filing and implementation frictions limit receipt among low-income families.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2674:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, SNAP, housing subsidies, work expenses, medical expenses, and geographic housing adjustments\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2675:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TCJA-style extension avoids a large scheduled child tax credit drop but does not recreate the fully refundable 2021 expansion\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:3152:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: the relevant prior is not a 2022-2024 child series because the draft evidence did not fetch the 2024 child value. I use 2022 and 2023 child SPM values plus the 2024 overall SPM signal. After the 2021 refundable-credit expansion expired, child SPM returned to low-to-mid teens, with 2021 at 5.2% treated as an exceptional policy year.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:3164:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Policy mechanism: under the condition, a House-style TCJA extension prevents the child credit from falling back toward the pre-TCJA $1,000 structure, which is poverty-reducing versus expiration. But the extension is not the 2021 fully refundable CTC and therefore does much less for the lowest-income children than the ARPA design that produced the 5.2% child SPM rate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:3168:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: if the enacted package preserves more SNAP, Medicaid-linked expense protection, or refundable child credit value than assumed, child SPM could fall toward 11%. Conversely, deeper nutrition cuts, weak employment, or high shelter inflation by 2028 could push the first print above 18%.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:3172:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Start with a post-expansion base of 13.0% to 13.3% from 12.4% in 2022, 13.7% in 2023, and the 12.9% overall SPM signal for 2024. Add about 0.4 percentage point for partial-refundable-credit limits versus a stronger anti-poverty design and about 0.5 point for likely transfer and expense headwinds under the House framework by 2028, giving 14.2%. The interval 10.8% to 18.0% allows roughly -3.4 to +3.8 points around the point, wider than the observed 2022-to-2023 child SPM move of 1.3 points because the horizon is four years, the target is conditional on fiscal legislation, and labor, housing, and medical-expense shocks can compound.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:8506:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau official poverty measure for calendar year 2025, reported as the percentage of people in poverty in the first Poverty in the United States: 2025 release/Table A-1 from CPS ASEC. This is not the SPM: taxes, refundable credits, SNAP, housing-cost adjustments, work expenses, medical expenses, and geographic threshold adjustments do not define this official-poverty target.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:9999:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Census reported Social Security moved 28.7 million people out of SPM poverty in 2024 and medical expenses moved more than 7.0 million people into SPM poverty in 2024; these figures matter less directly for children than refundable credits, SNAP, housing, child care, and payroll-tax effects.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10023:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: upside risk is meaningful if CPS ASEC measured earnings for low-income families disappoint, housing and child care expenses push more families below SPM thresholds, or benefit participation/resource measurement weakens. Downside risk comes from stronger low-wage earnings, lower inflation thresholds, and larger refundable-credit effects than expected.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10031:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Scenario bounds: a low-side case near 11.7 percent would require strong low-income family earnings and favorable refundable-credit or benefit measurement; a high-side case near 15.4 percent would require weak measured earnings plus housing, child care, medical, or work-expense effects that reduce SPM resources. Values outside the interval would likely need either a recession-scale income miss or an unexpectedly large policy/resource effect.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10058:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, medical expenses, work expenses, and geographic housing-cost adjustments\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10060:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10434:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, and noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10466:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: absent a new 2021-scale refundable-credit expansion, the most relevant outside-view level is the post-expiration 2022-2024 range of 12.4 to 13.8 percent, with more weight on 2023-2024 because SPM thresholds, rent, medical expenses, and benefit take-up had mostly normalized after the pandemic emergency period.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10474:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:10478:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate above 17 percent, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13148:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tariff receipts are elevated but refund implementation adds uncertainty\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13169:          \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/stock-market-cpi-inflation-06-10-2026/card/tariff-refunds-equaled-tariff-collections-last-month-5qhm8A4nfvk2eFlKU9kB\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13275:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched May 2026 Treasury-reported context: customs duties collected were 22 USD billion, refunds were 22 USD billion, fiscal-year net tariff revenue through May was 189 USD billion versus 81 USD billion a year earlier, and the calendar-adjusted deficit was 2 percent or 24 USD billion below the prior year.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13282:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched June 2025 context reporting Treasury tariff revenue around 27 to 30 USD billion in June 2025, up from 4 USD billion a year earlier, and fiscal-year tariff revenue above 113 USD billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13290:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"I start with a recent-June base of about (27 + 66) / 2 = 46.5 billion, add roughly 20 billion for higher interest and entitlement outlays, add 10 billion for tariff-refund and policy noise, and round to a 72 billion point. The 80% interval is set wide at -35 to 210 billion to cover a possible June surplus from strong receipts and a 2023-like high-deficit outlier if payment timing or refunds raise outlays.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13294:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: elevated tariff receipts and June tax collections could produce a small surplus, especially if refunds are not concentrated in June, which is why the lower bound is negative despite the point being a deficit.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13318:        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tariff-refund shock reduces a revenue tailwind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13439:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June 2025 receipts were 526.445 billion, outlays were 499.435 billion, and the deficit/surplus was -27.010 billion; FYTD receipts were 4008.146 billion, FYTD outlays were 5345.518 billion, and FYTD deficit was 1337.372 billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13446:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June 2024 receipts were 466.255 billion, outlays were 532.220 billion, and the deficit was 65.965 billion; June 2023 receipts were 418.317 billion, outlays were 646.085 billion, and the deficit was 227.768 billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13453:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June 2022 receipts were 460.761 billion, outlays were 549.603 billion, and the deficit was 88.842 billion; FYTD deficit through June 2022 was 515.067 billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13460:            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Reports cited Treasury figures that May 2026 net tariff revenue was 0 billion after 22 billion collected and 22 billion refunded, FYTD net tariff revenue was 189 billion versus 81 billion a year earlier, adjusted FYTD deficit was 2 percent or 24 billion below the prior year, and Treasury's April-June 2026 borrowing estimate was 189 billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13468:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: if June receipts remain strong and tariff revenues are not fully offset by refunds, the deficit could land near the low end or even close to balance; however, June 1, 2026 is a Monday, so the payment-shift suppression visible in 2024 and 2025 should not repeat.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:13472:          text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Start from a recent-June base rate around 90 billion. Add roughly 55 billion for normal June benefit and Medicare/HHS payments not shifted into May, add about 25 billion for higher interest and entitlement growth, and subtract about 15 billion for still-elevated customs/tariff receipts net of refunds: 90 + 55 + 25 - 15 = 155 billion. Set an 80% interval of 20 to 310 billion to cover tax-payment and refund volatility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:5:    slug: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:7:    title: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Individual income tax refunds, FY2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:9:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"What will total federal individual income tax refunds be in fiscal year 2026 as reported by the U.S. Treasury?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:19:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to total individual income tax refunds for fiscal year 2026 in the final Monthly Treasury Statement for September 2026. If Treasury revises the table after the initial final MTS release, the first final fiscal-year table governs.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:20:    dataPointId: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"treasury.mts.individual_income_tax_refunds.fy2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:38:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Refunds resolve quickly through the Monthly Treasury Statement and give Thesis Institute agents a tax-administration target that connects filing behavior, withholding, and refundable-credit policy.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:43:        call: 'treasury.lookup({ table: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"monthly_treasury_statement\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", series: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"individual_income_tax_refunds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", fiscal_years: [2021, 2025] })',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:49:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ year: 2026, output: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"individual_income_tax_refunds_cash\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", timing: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fiscal_year\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", map_to: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tax_unit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" })',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:51:          '{ point: 482, ci80: [454, 520], drivers: [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ctc_refundability\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"eitc_claiming\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"withholding_gap\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"] }',\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:55:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The central estimate keeps refunds slightly below the FY2024 spike but above FY2025. The interval widens for filing-season timing because a few weeks of delayed refunds can move dollars across fiscal years without changing tax-year liability.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:74:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"IRS Statistics of Income, Affordable Care Act individual income tax items\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:76:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the IRS SOI tax-year 2025 estimate for net premium tax credit reconciliation on individual income tax returns, defined as additional net PTC claimed minus excess advance PTC repaid. If IRS publishes separate line-item and ACA-statistics estimates, the ACA-statistics table governs.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:122:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"How many individual income tax returns will claim the Saver's Credit for tax year 2025 in IRS Statistics of Income data?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/tax.ts:168:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The forecast expects mild growth as real earnings stabilize and contribution rates recover, but it keeps the lower tail near the recent trough because the credit remains nonrefundable and low-salience.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/raw_response.txt:1:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"country\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"US\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"US June 2026 Monthly Treasury Deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"What monthly deficit will the U.S. Treasury Bureau of the Fiscal Service report in the first-print Monthly Treasury Statement for June 2026, Table 1 summary of receipts, outlays, and deficit/surplus, not seasonally adjusted?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"unit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"usd_billions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pointEstimate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":72,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":-35,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":210,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"confidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0.8,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resolutionDate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-07-13\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resolutionSource\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Official agency release\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resolutionRule\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolve to the first Monthly Treasury Statement print for June 2026, Table 1 deficit/surplus for the month of June 2026, with deficit positive and surplus negative, converted from the agency's dollars-in-millions presentation to USD billions and rounded to the nearest 1 billion. Ignore later revisions or annual-report restatements.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"dataPointId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"treasury.mts.table_1.deficit_surplus.2026-06.first_print\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"historicalContext\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2025-06 monthly deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":27},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2024-06 monthly deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":66},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2023-06 monthly deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":228},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-05 tariff refunds noted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":22},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026 fiscal-year tariff revenue through May\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":189}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"drivers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June quarterly tax payments usually lift receipts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"June 1 payment timing can move outlays across months\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tariff receipts are elevated but refund implementation adds uncertainty\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"interest and entitlement outlays remain high\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"FY2026 deficit was reported slightly below FY2025 through May after calendar adjustment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sourceContext\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/release-calendar/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/stock-market-cpi-inflation-06-10-2026/card/tariff-refunds-equaled-tariff-collections-last-month-5qhm8A4nfvk2eFlKU9kB\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://www.kiplinger.com/taxes/tariff-stimulus-checks\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"runAt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-06-17T02:35:38Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reasoning\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Forecast for the June 2026 Monthly Treasury Statement deficit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolver is the first official Bureau of the Fiscal Service Monthly Treasury Statement print for June 2026, using the monthly deficit/surplus in Table 1 and treating deficits as positive USD billions.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Checked the Treasury Fiscal Data release calendar page for the release-calendar source used to verify the official schedule surface.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched official Fiscal Data release-calendar page showing Release Calendar, copyright 2026, and the statement that it shows estimated dates and times for upcoming data releases; June 2026 MTS is scheduled for 2026-07-13.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Checked the Treasury Fiscal Data Monthly Treasury Statement dataset page as the official resolution source.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched official Monthly Treasury Statement dataset URL for the Table 1 resolver; historical Table 1 June monthly deficits used as reference points were 2025-06: 27 USD billion, 2024-06: 66 USD billion, and 2023-06: 228 USD billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"news.lookup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Checked contemporaneous reporting on the May 2026 Treasury statement for current fiscal context.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched May 2026 Treasury-reported context: customs duties collected were 22 USD billion, refunds were 22 USD billion, fiscal-year net tariff revenue through May was 189 USD billion versus 81 USD billion a year earlier, and the calendar-adjusted deficit was 2 percent or 24 USD billion below the prior year.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"history.lookup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Checked reporting around June 2025 tariff and fiscal context as a prior-year anchor.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched June 2025 context reporting Treasury tariff revenue around 27 to 30 USD billion in June 2025, up from 4 USD billion a year earlier, and fiscal-year tariff revenue above 113 USD billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Base-rate step: the most relevant reference class is recent June MTS prints because June contains quarterly estimated tax payments and has strong seasonality. The recent June deficits of 27, 66, and 228 billion imply a wide but positive-deficit center; the two most recent years point closer to 50 billion than to the 2023 outlier.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"I start with a recent-June base of about (27 + 66) / 2 = 46.5 billion, add roughly 20 billion for higher interest and entitlement outlays, add 10 billion for tariff-refund and policy noise, and round to a 72 billion point. The 80% interval is set wide at -35 to 210 billion to cover a possible June surplus from strong receipts and a 2023-like high-deficit outlier if payment timing or refunds raise outlays.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: elevated tariff receipts and June tax collections could produce a small surplus, especially if refunds are not concentrated in June, which is why the lower bound is negative despite the point being a deficit.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":72,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":-35,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":210}]}\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:180:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Conditional on no materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for tax year 2026, what will the U.S. infant mortality rate be for calendar year 2026?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:191:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the final U.S. infant mortality rate for calendar year 2026, in infant deaths per 1,000 live births, from CDC/NCHS NVSS linked birth/infant death records, conditional on no materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for TY2026. If such a policy is in effect, this baseline scenario is marked unresolved.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:193:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"No materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit in effect for TY2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:208:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"No large refundable CTC income shock\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:214:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This cell targets the final NCHS/NVSS linked birth/infant death rate: deaths under age 1 per 1,000 live births. The baseline scenario is current-law CTC policy, so a $3,000 fully refundable CTC would make this scenario unresolved rather than wrong.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:238:    slug: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"infant-mortality-rate-2026-ctc-3000-refundable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:240:    title: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Infant mortality 2026 | $3,000 fully refundable CTC\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:242:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Conditional on a $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for tax year 2026, what will the U.S. infant mortality rate be for calendar year 2026?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:253:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resolves to the final U.S. infant mortality rate for calendar year 2026, in infant deaths per 1,000 live births, from CDC/NCHS NVSS linked birth/infant death records, conditional on a materially equivalent $3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit being in effect for TY2026. If the policy is not in effect, this scenario is marked unresolved.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:255:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$3,000 fully refundable Child Tax Credit in effect for TY2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:257:      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cdc.nchs.nvss.infant_mortality_rate.2026.ctc_3000_refundable.final\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:258:    policyParameter: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"irc.24.ctc_3000_fully_refundable.2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:278:        text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This scenario asks about the observed national infant mortality rate if the $3,000 fully refundable CTC is actually in force for TY2026. The official NCHS rate still resolves the value; the condition determines whether this scenario is active.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nsite/src/data/almanac-examples/health.ts:282:        call: 'policyengine.simulate({ reform: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ctc_3000_fully_refundable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", year: 2026, output: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cash_transfer_to_households_with_infants\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", map_to: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"household\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" })',\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:32:    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026-05 tariff refunds noted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:36:    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2026 fiscal-year tariff revenue through May\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:43:   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tariff receipts are elevated but refund implementation adds uncertainty\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:50:   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/stock-market-cpi-inflation-06-10-2026/card/tariff-refunds-equaled-tariff-collections-last-month-5qhm8A4nfvk2eFlKU9kB\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:79:    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched May 2026 Treasury-reported context: customs duties collected were 22 USD billion, refunds were 22 USD billion, fiscal-year net tariff revenue through May was 189 USD billion versus 81 USD billion a year earlier, and the calendar-adjusted deficit was 2 percent or 24 USD billion below the prior year.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:85:    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fetched June 2025 context reporting Treasury tariff revenue around 27 to 30 USD billion in June 2025, up from 4 USD billion a year earlier, and fiscal-year tariff revenue above 113 USD billion.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:93:    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"I start with a recent-June base of about (27 + 66) / 2 = 46.5 billion, add roughly 20 billion for higher interest and entitlement outlays, add 10 billion for tariff-refund and policy noise, and round to a 72 billion point. The 80% interval is set wide at -35 to 210 billion to cover a possible June surplus from strong receipts and a 2023-like high-deficit outlier if payment timing or refunds raise outlays.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-35-28z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:97:    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: elevated tariff receipts and June tax collections could produce a small surplus, especially if refunds are not concentrated in June, which is why the lower bound is negative despite the point being a deficit.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\": 0, \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-17-54z-treasury-mts-individual-income-tax-refunds-fy2026-fy2026/draft_codex_events.jsonl:121:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ws_062c14b93c720bac016a405b3a16a08194991e9abddff53755\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"web_search\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"query\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"IRS filing season statistics week ending May 8 2026 total refunds 324.757 billion\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"action\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"search\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"query\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"IRS filing season statistics week ending May 8 2026 total refunds 324.757 billion\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"queries\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"IRS filing season statistics week ending May 8 2026 total refunds 324.757 billion\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"May 8, 2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"total refunds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"324.757\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" IRS\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"site:irs.gov \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"324,757\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"May 8, 2026\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"]}}}\\\\n\\\", \\\"exit_code\\\": 0, \\\"status\\\": \\\"completed\\\"}}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-24-50z-irs-filing-season-total-amount-refunded-october-2026-first-print-october-2026-first-print/draft_codex_events.jsonl:44:{\\\"type\\\": \\\"item.completed\\\", \\\"item\\\": {\\\"id\\\": \\\"ws_08e3969fff61ee57016a405c19fc188197a00b83ca44485117\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"web_search\\\", \\\"query\\\": \\\"National Taxpayer Advocate mid-year report Congress 2026 filing season refunds 17 percent dollars 90 million refunds\\\", \\\"action\\\": {\\\"type\\\": \\\"search\\\", \\\"query\\\": \\\"National Taxpayer Advocate mid-year report Congress 2026 filing season refunds 17 percent dollars 90 million refunds\\\", \\\"queries\\\": [\\\"National Taxpayer Advocate mid-year report Congress 2026 filing season refunds 17 percent dollars 90 million refunds\\\", \\\"site:taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov 2026 filing season refunds 17 percent dollars 90 million\\\", \\\"site:taxpayeradvocate.irs.gov/reports 2027 Objectives Report to Congress 2026 filing season refunds\\\"]}}}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/draft_codex_last_message.txt:1:{\\\"slug\\\":\\\"census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027\\\",\\\"country\\\":\\\"US\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"data\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"US child SPM poverty rate, 2027\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027, first print in the Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release.\\\",\\\"unit\\\":\\\"percent\\\",\\\"pointEstimate\\\":14.2,\\\"ciLow\\\":11.5,\\\"ciHigh\\\":17.4,\\\"confidence\\\":0.8,\\\"resolutionDate\\\":\\\"2028-09-12\\\",\\\"resolutionSource\\\":\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release and CPS ASEC Supplemental Poverty Measure table B-2\\\",\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\":\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\",\\\"resolutionRule\\\":\\\"Resolve to the first U.S. Census Bureau published calendar-year 2027 Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18, reported in percent, usually table B-2 of the annual Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release. Use the first print rounded to one decimal place; ignore later CPS ASEC, SPM-methodology, or table revisions unless the original release is formally corrected before the same release day ends.\\\",\\\"dataPointId\\\":\\\"census.cps_asec.spm.child_under18_poverty_rate.2027.first_print\\\",\\\"historicalContext\\\":[{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2024 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":13.8},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2023 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":13.7},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2022 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":12.4},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2021 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":5.2},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2019 pre-pandemic child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":12.6}],\\\"drivers\\\":[\\\"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, medical expenses, work expenses, and geographic housing-cost adjustments\\\",\\\"recent post-pandemic child SPM rates have re-centered near 13 to 14 percent after the temporary 2021 child tax credit expansion expired\\\",\\\"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\\\",\\\"first-print CPS ASEC sampling and processing noise warrants a wide interval even without a structural policy shift\\\"],\\\"sourceContext\\\":[\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2025/demo/p60-287.html\\\",\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\\\",\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-283.html\\\",\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\\\",\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\"],\\\"runAt\\\":\\\"2026-06-27T14:15:49Z\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":[{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"heading\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Forecast for calendar-year 2027 child Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Census annual income, poverty, and health insurance release pages and CPS ASEC SPM table B-2 for the recent child SPM reference class.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched child under-18 SPM poverty rates: 2024 = 13.8 percent, 2023 = 13.7 percent, 2022 = 12.4 percent, and 2021 = 5.2 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked the 2025 Census Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release page and linked SPM table for the latest first-print benchmark.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched latest first-print calendar-year 2024 child SPM poverty rate = 13.8 percent; all-person SPM poverty rate in the same release = 12.9 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Census historical SPM table/report context for pre-pandemic and pandemic-policy anchor points.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched pre-pandemic 2019 child SPM poverty rate = 12.6 percent and temporary-credit 2021 child SPM poverty rate = 5.2 percent, showing a 7.4 percentage-point policy-sensitive drop versus 2019.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked the Census newsroom release calendar/annual income-poverty release schedule for the first-print release timing.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Calendar check used the Census release calendar and annual September income-poverty release pattern; the target first-print resolution date recorded for this run is 2028-09-12, with prior annual September first-print releases including 2025-09-09 for calendar-year 2024 data and 2024-09-10 for calendar-year 2023 data.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: absent a new 2021-scale refundable-credit expansion, the most relevant outside-view level is the post-expiration 2022-2024 range of 12.4 to 13.8 percent, with more weight on 2023-2024 because SPM thresholds, rent, medical expenses, and benefit take-up had mostly normalized after the pandemic emergency period.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Level and momentum: the recent level is already slightly above the 2019 pre-pandemic rate of 12.6 percent, and the 2023 to 2024 movement was essentially flat at +0.1 percentage point. That argues against extrapolating a large trend increase from the 2021-to-2022 jump, because that jump mostly reflected policy expiration rather than ordinary poverty momentum.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate into the upper teens, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Point calculation: start from a weighted recent baseline of 13.7 percent using 2022-2024 values, add +0.3 for loss of residual pandemic-era supports and higher SPM expense pressure by 2027, add +0.2 for recession/policy asymmetry because downside poverty risks are larger than upside income surprises, giving 14.2 percent. Interval: realized first-print child SPM swings include 2021 to 2022 = +7.2 pp and 2022 to 2024 = +1.4 pp; excluding the extreme temporary-credit reversal, a normal 80 percent band around 14.2 is about +/-2.5 pp, widened and right-skewed for policy/labor risk to 11.5 to 17.4.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"forecast\\\",\\\"point\\\":14.2,\\\"ciLow\\\":11.5,\\\"ciHigh\\\":17.4}]}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:3:    \\\"slug\\\": \\\"census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:6:    \\\"title\\\": \\\"US child SPM poverty rate, 2027\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:7:    \\\"question\\\": \\\"U.S. Census Bureau Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027, first print in the Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release.\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:16:    \\\"resolutionRule\\\": \\\"Resolve to the first U.S. Census Bureau published calendar-year 2027 Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18, reported in percent in the annual Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release. Use Table B-2 if that table remains the SPM age, race, and Hispanic-origin table; if table numbering or format changes, use the equivalent first-print Census SPM table for age group under 18. Use the first print rounded to one decimal place; ignore later CPS ASEC, SPM-methodology, or table revisions unless the original release is formally corrected before the same release day ends. The 2028 Census release calendar entry was not yet available at this run, so this date is an operational end-of-September resolution deadline, with the value resolved on the actual first official print when posted.\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:17:    \\\"dataPointId\\\": \\\"census.cps_asec.spm.child_under18_poverty_rate.2027.first_print\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:43:      \\\"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:57:        \\\"text\\\": \\\"Forecast for calendar-year 2027 child Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:61:        \\\"text\\\": \\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, and noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:85:        \\\"result\\\": \\\"Fetched annual first-print publication dates of 2025-09-09 for calendar-year 2024 data and 2024-09-10 for calendar-year 2023 data; no official 2028 calendar entry for calendar-year 2027 poverty data was available in the checked Census calendar at run time.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:97:        \\\"text\\\": \\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:101:        \\\"text\\\": \\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate above 17 percent, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:105:        \\\"text\\\": \\\"Point calculation: start from a weighted recent baseline of 13.7 percent using 2022-2024 values, add +0.3 for loss of residual pandemic-era supports and higher SPM expense pressure by 2027, add +0.2 for recession/policy asymmetry because downside poverty risks are larger than upside income surprises, giving 14.2 percent. Interval: using the non-2021-reversal post-expiration evidence, 2022 to 2023 was +1.3 pp and 2023 to 2024 was +0.1 pp, while 2019 to 2024 was +1.2 pp; I treat roughly +/-2.5 pp as normal 80 percent uncertainty around 14.2, then widen and right-skew judgmentally for tax-credit, benefit, and labor-market tail risk to 11.5 to 17.4.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:3:  \\\"slug\\\": \\\"census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:6:  \\\"title\\\": \\\"US child SPM poverty rate, 2027\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:7:  \\\"question\\\": \\\"U.S. Census Bureau Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027, first print in the Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release.\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:16:  \\\"resolutionRule\\\": \\\"Resolve to the first U.S. Census Bureau published calendar-year 2027 Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18, reported in percent in the annual Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release. Use Table B-2 if that table remains the SPM age, race, and Hispanic-origin table; if table numbering or format changes, use the equivalent first-print Census SPM table for age group under 18. Use the first print rounded to one decimal place; ignore later CPS ASEC, SPM-methodology, or table revisions unless the original release is formally corrected before the same release day ends. The 2028 Census release calendar entry was not yet available at this run, so this date is an operational end-of-September resolution deadline, with the value resolved on the actual first official print when posted.\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:17:  \\\"dataPointId\\\": \\\"census.cps_asec.spm.child_under18_poverty_rate.2027.first_print\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:43:   \\\"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:57:    \\\"text\\\": \\\"Forecast for calendar-year 2027 child Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:61:    \\\"text\\\": \\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, and noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:85:    \\\"result\\\": \\\"Fetched annual first-print publication dates of 2025-09-09 for calendar-year 2024 data and 2024-09-10 for calendar-year 2023 data; no official 2028 calendar entry for calendar-year 2027 poverty data was available in the checked Census calendar at run time.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:97:    \\\"text\\\": \\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:101:    \\\"text\\\": \\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate above 17 percent, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:105:    \\\"text\\\": \\\"Point calculation: start from a weighted recent baseline of 13.7 percent using 2022-2024 values, add +0.3 for loss of residual pandemic-era supports and higher SPM expense pressure by 2027, add +0.2 for recession/policy asymmetry because downside poverty risks are larger than upside income surprises, giving 14.2 percent. Interval: using the non-2021-reversal post-expiration evidence, 2022 to 2023 was +1.3 pp and 2023 to 2024 was +0.1 pp, while 2019 to 2024 was +1.2 pp; I treat roughly +/-2.5 pp as normal 80 percent uncertainty around 14.2, then widen and right-skew judgmentally for tax-credit, benefit, and labor-market tail risk to 11.5 to 17.4.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/pre_submit_review_prompt.md:6:- series: census.spm.child_poverty_rate.2027\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/pre_submit_review_prompt.md:7:- period: calendar year 2027\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/pre_submit_review_prompt.md:40:{\\\"slug\\\":\\\"census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027\\\",\\\"country\\\":\\\"US\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"data\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"US child SPM poverty rate, 2027\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027, first print in the Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release.\\\",\\\"unit\\\":\\\"percent\\\",\\\"pointEstimate\\\":14.2,\\\"ciLow\\\":11.5,\\\"ciHigh\\\":17.4,\\\"confidence\\\":0.8,\\\"resolutionDate\\\":\\\"2028-09-12\\\",\\\"resolutionSource\\\":\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release and CPS ASEC Supplemental Poverty Measure table B-2\\\",\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\":\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\",\\\"resolutionRule\\\":\\\"Resolve to the first U.S. Census Bureau published calendar-year 2027 Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18, reported in percent, usually table B-2 of the annual Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release. Use the first print rounded to one decimal place; ignore later CPS ASEC, SPM-methodology, or table revisions unless the original release is formally corrected before the same release day ends.\\\",\\\"dataPointId\\\":\\\"census.cps_asec.spm.child_under18_poverty_rate.2027.first_print\\\",\\\"historicalContext\\\":[{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2024 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":13.8},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2023 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":13.7},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2022 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":12.4},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2021 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":5.2},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2019 pre-pandemic child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":12.6}],\\\"drivers\\\":[\\\"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, medical expenses, work expenses, and geographic housing-cost adjustments\\\",\\\"recent post-pandemic child SPM rates have re-centered near 13 to 14 percent after the temporary 2021 child tax credit expansion expired\\\",\\\"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\\\",\\\"first-print CPS ASEC sampling and processing noise warrants a wide interval even without a structural policy shift\\\"],\\\"sourceContext\\\":[\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2025/demo/p60-287.html\\\",\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\\\",\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-283.html\\\",\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\\\",\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\"],\\\"runAt\\\":\\\"2026-06-27T14:15:49Z\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":[{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"heading\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Forecast for calendar-year 2027 child Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Census annual income, poverty, and health insurance release pages and CPS ASEC SPM table B-2 for the recent child SPM reference class.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched child under-18 SPM poverty rates: 2024 = 13.8 percent, 2023 = 13.7 percent, 2022 = 12.4 percent, and 2021 = 5.2 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked the 2025 Census Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release page and linked SPM table for the latest first-print benchmark.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched latest first-print calendar-year 2024 child SPM poverty rate = 13.8 percent; all-person SPM poverty rate in the same release = 12.9 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Census historical SPM table/report context for pre-pandemic and pandemic-policy anchor points.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched pre-pandemic 2019 child SPM poverty rate = 12.6 percent and temporary-credit 2021 child SPM poverty rate = 5.2 percent, showing a 7.4 percentage-point policy-sensitive drop versus 2019.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked the Census newsroom release calendar/annual income-poverty release schedule for the first-print release timing.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Calendar check used the Census release calendar and annual September income-poverty release pattern; the target first-print resolution date recorded for this run is 2028-09-12, with prior annual September first-print releases including 2025-09-09 for calendar-year 2024 data and 2024-09-10 for calendar-year 2023 data.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: absent a new 2021-scale refundable-credit expansion, the most relevant outside-view level is the post-expiration 2022-2024 range of 12.4 to 13.8 percent, with more weight on 2023-2024 because SPM thresholds, rent, medical expenses, and benefit take-up had mostly normalized after the pandemic emergency period.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Level and momentum: the recent level is already slightly above the 2019 pre-pandemic rate of 12.6 percent, and the 2023 to 2024 movement was essentially flat at +0.1 percentage point. That argues against extrapolating a large trend increase from the 2021-to-2022 jump, because that jump mostly reflected policy expiration rather than ordinary poverty momentum.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate into the upper teens, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Point calculation: start from a weighted recent baseline of 13.7 percent using 2022-2024 values, add +0.3 for loss of residual pandemic-era supports and higher SPM expense pressure by 2027, add +0.2 for recession/policy asymmetry because downside poverty risks are larger than upside income surprises, giving 14.2 percent. Interval: realized first-print child SPM swings include 2021 to 2022 = +7.2 pp and 2022 to 2024 = +1.4 pp; excluding the extreme temporary-credit reversal, a normal 80 percent band around 14.2 is about +/-2.5 pp, widened and right-skewed for policy/labor risk to 11.5 to 17.4.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"forecast\\\",\\\"point\\\":14.2,\\\"ciLow\\\":11.5,\\\"ciHigh\\\":17.4}]}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t12-54-48z-statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04/normalized_cells.json:69:    \\\"call\\\": \\\"Checked Statistics Canada 2026-2027 major economic release dates PDF for Gross domestic product by industry.\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t12-54-48z-statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04/raw_response.txt:1:{\\\"slug\\\":\\\"statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04\\\",\\\"country\\\":\\\"CA\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"data\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Canada GDP by industry, Apr 2026\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"What will Statistics Canada first report as the month-over-month percent change in seasonally adjusted real gross domestic product by industry, all industries, at basic prices, for April 2026?\\\",\\\"unit\\\":\\\"percent_growth\\\",\\\"pointEstimate\\\":0.4,\\\"ciLow\\\":0.1,\\\"ciHigh\\\":0.7,\\\"confidence\\\":0.8,\\\"resolutionDate\\\":\\\"2026-06-30\\\",\\\"resolutionSource\\\":\\\"Statistics Canada Table 36-10-0434-01 and The Daily release for Gross domestic product by industry, April 2026\\\",\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\":\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/t1/tbl1/en/tv.action?pid=3610043401\\\",\\\"resolutionRule\\\":\\\"Resolve to the first Statistics Canada publication on June 30, 2026 for April 2026 seasonally adjusted real GDP by industry, all industries, month-to-month percent change, rounded to one decimal percentage point. If the June 30, 2026 Daily text and Table 36-10-0434-01 disagree at first publication, use the table value for all industries month-to-month percent change. Ignore later revisions.\\\",\\\"dataPointId\\\":\\\"statcan.36-10-0434-01.all_industries.month_to_month_percent_change.2026-04.first_print\\\",\\\"historicalContext\\\":[{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2026-03 first print\\\",\\\"value\\\":-0.1},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2026-02 first print\\\",\\\"value\\\":0.2},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2026-01 first print\\\",\\\"value\\\":0},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2026-04 advance estimate\\\",\\\"value\\\":0.4},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2025-12 first print\\\",\\\"value\\\":0.2}],\\\"drivers\\\":[\\\"Statistics Canada advance estimate points to 0.4 percent April growth\\\",\\\"Mining, oil and gas rebound after March contraction supports upside\\\",\\\"Manufacturing and transportation strength support goods momentum\\\",\\\"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting cited as an offset\\\",\\\"Recent monthly first prints cluster near 0.0 to 0.2 percent\\\"],\\\"sourceContext\\\":[\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/release-diffusion/2026-eng.pdf\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/t1/tbl1/en/tv.action?pid=3610043401\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260529/dq260529b-eng.htm\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260529/t001b-eng.htm\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260430/dq260430a-eng.htm\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260331/dq260331a-eng.htm\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260227/dq260227b-eng.htm\\\"],\\\"runAt\\\":\\\"2026-06-27T12:54:53Z\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":[{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"heading\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Forecast for Canada April 2026 real GDP by industry monthly growth\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Resolver is the first Statistics Canada print for seasonally adjusted real GDP by industry, all industries, month-to-month percent change for April 2026. The target is the June 30, 2026 Daily/table release, not later revised history.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Statistics Canada 2026-2027 major economic release dates PDF for Gross domestic product by industry.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"The schedule lists Gross domestic product by industry for April 2026 with release date June 30, 2026; adjacent GDP by industry releases are May 29, 2026 for March 2026 and July 31, 2026 for May 2026.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Fetched Statistics Canada The Daily release for Gross domestic product by industry, March 2026.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"The March 2026 release reported real GDP by industry at -0.1% month to month, after February +0.2%, and gave an April 2026 advance estimate of +0.4%; goods-producing industries were -0.8% in March and services-producing industries were +0.1%.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Fetched Statistics Canada table attached to the March 2026 Daily release.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Table 1 shows all industries month-to-month changes of -0.3 for October 2025, 0.1 for November 2025, 0.1 for December 2025, -0.0 for January 2026, 0.2 for February 2026, and -0.1 for March 2026; March 2026 annual-rate level was 2,339,731 million dollars.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Fetched prior Statistics Canada The Daily releases for January, February, and December context.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"January 2026 table context was -0.0%, February 2026 first text print was +0.2%, and December 2025 first text print was +0.2%; the February release's advance estimate for March was essentially unchanged, later first printed as -0.1%.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Base rate/reference class: recent official first prints around this series are small positives, with December +0.2, January -0.0, February +0.2, and March -0.1. The simple recent first-print average is about +0.1, but the agency's own April advance estimate is a strong near-term signal and usually deserves heavy weight.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Inside-view decomposition: level effects are modest because Q1 GDP by industry was only +0.1 for the quarter. Momentum is mixed after March weakness, but the named April advance components point to rebounds in mining, quarrying, oil and gas extraction, manufacturing, and transportation and warehousing. One-off factors include March maintenance/weather disruptions in energy and auto-related volatility that can reverse. Policy/trade mechanisms remain a drag risk through tariffs and manufacturing/export uncertainty.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration: the +0.4 advance estimate could be revised down if the March rebound in goods sectors was overstated, if agriculture/forestry/fishing weakness is larger than early information suggested, or if services activity failed to keep pace; an outside downside scenario is a flat or negative first print near 0.0 or below.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"I anchor at the recent first-print base rate near +0.1, put most weight on StatCan's April advance estimate of +0.4, and choose 0.4 as the rounded point. No separate ARIMA or ETS model was fit; the model prior is a recent-first-print persistence average updated toward the official advance estimate. The 80% interval is for the one-decimal rounded first print. Recent advance-to-first-print misses in visible releases were about 0.0 to 0.1 percentage point, but sector volatility and tariff/energy uncertainty justify a wider 80% interval of 0.1 to 0.7. Upside outside the interval would require a broad mining/manufacturing surge above +0.7; downside outside the interval would require advance-estimate reversal to 0.0 or lower.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Review disposition: accepted the resolver URL fix by using the official Table 36-10-0434-01 endpoint until the June 30 Daily URL exists, accepted the first-publication tie-break by making the table control if text and table disagree, and reconciled January 2026 context to -0.0/0.0 throughout. Also accepted the optional clarification that this is a rounded first-print interval and not a separately fit time-series model.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"forecast\\\",\\\"point\\\":0.4,\\\"ciLow\\\":0.1,\\\"ciHigh\\\":0.7}]}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t12-54-48z-statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04/draft_codex_stdout.jsonl:29:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_13\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"country\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CA\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Canada GDP by industry, Apr 2026\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"What will Statistics Canada first report as the month-over-month percent change in seasonally adjusted real gross domestic product by industry, all industries, at basic prices, for April 2026?\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"unit\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"percent_growth\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"pointEstimate\\\\\\\":0.4,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":0.1,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":0.7,\\\\\\\"confidence\\\\\\\":0.8,\\\\\\\"resolutionDate\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-30\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionSource\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Statistics Canada The Daily release and Table 36-10-0434-01\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260529/dq260529b-eng.htm\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionRule\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Resolve to the first Statistics Canada print released on June 30, 2026 for April 2026 seasonally adjusted real GDP by industry, all industries, month-to-month percent change, rounded to one decimal percentage point. Ignore later revisions and use the first Daily/table value if the text and table differ after subsequent updates.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"dataPointId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"statcan.36-10-0434-01.all_industries.month_to_month_percent_change.2026-04.first_print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"historicalContext\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-03 first print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":-0.1},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-02 first print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":0.2},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-01 first print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":0.1},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-04 advance estimate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":0.4},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2025-12 first print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":0.2}],\\\\\\\"drivers\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"Statistics Canada advance estimate points to 0.4 percent April growth\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Mining, oil and gas rebound after March contraction supports upside\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Manufacturing and transportation strength support goods momentum\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting cited as an offset\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Recent monthly first prints cluster near 0.1 to 0.2 percent\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"sourceContext\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/release-diffusion/2026-eng.pdf\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260529/dq260529b-eng.htm\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260529/t001b-eng.htm\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260430/dq260430a-eng.htm\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260331/dq260331a-eng.htm\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260227/dq260227b-eng.htm\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"runAt\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-27T12:54:53Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"reasoning\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Forecast for Canada April 2026 real GDP by industry monthly growth\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Resolver is the first Statistics Canada print for seasonally adjusted real GDP by industry, all industries, month-to-month percent change for April 2026. The target is the June 30, 2026 Daily/table release, not later revised history.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked Statistics Canada 2026-2027 major economic release dates PDF for Gross domestic product by industry.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The schedule lists Gross domestic product by industry for April 2026 with release date June 30, 2026; adjacent GDP by industry releases are May 29, 2026 for March 2026 and July 31, 2026 for May 2026.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched Statistics Canada The Daily release for Gross domestic product by industry, March 2026.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The March 2026 release reported real GDP by industry at -0.1% month to month, after February +0.2%, and gave an April 2026 advance estimate of +0.4%; goods-producing industries were -0.8% in March and services-producing industries were +0.1%.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched Statistics Canada table attached to the March 2026 Daily release.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Table 1 shows all industries month-to-month changes of -0.3 for October 2025, 0.1 for November 2025, 0.1 for December 2025, -0.0 for January 2026, 0.2 for February 2026, and -0.1 for March 2026; March 2026 annual-rate level was 2,339,731 million dollars.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched prior Statistics Canada The Daily releases for January, February, and December context.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"January 2026 first text print was +0.1%, February 2026 first text print was +0.2%, and December 2025 first text print was +0.2%; the February release's advance estimate for March was essentially unchanged, later first printed as -0.1%.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Base rate/reference class: recent official first prints around this series are small positives, with December +0.2, January +0.1, February +0.2, and March -0.1. The simple recent first-print average is about +0.1, but the agency's own April advance estimate is a strong near-term signal and usually deserves heavy weight.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Inside-view decomposition: level effects are modest because Q1 GDP by industry was only +0.1 for the quarter. Momentum is mixed after March weakness, but the named April advance components point to rebounds in mining, quarrying, oil and gas extraction, manufacturing, and transportation and warehousing. One-off factors include March maintenance/weather disruptions in energy and auto-related volatility that can reverse. Policy/trade mechanisms remain a drag risk through tariffs and manufacturing/export uncertainty.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: the +0.4 advance estimate could be revised down if the March rebound in goods sectors was overstated, if agriculture/forestry/fishing weakness is larger than early information suggested, or if services activity failed to keep pace; an outside downside scenario is a flat or negative first print near 0.0 or below.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"I anchor at the recent first-print base rate near +0.1, put most weight on StatCan's April advance estimate of +0.4, and choose 0.4 as the rounded point. Recent advance-to-first-print misses in visible releases were about 0.0 to 0.1 percentage point, but sector volatility and tariff/energy uncertainty justify a wider 80% interval of 0.1 to 0.7. Upside outside the interval would require a broad mining/manufacturing surge above +0.7; downside outside the interval would require advance-estimate reversal to 0.0 or lower.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\":0.4,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":0.1,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":0.7}]}\\\"}}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/draft_codex_stdout.jsonl:39:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_18\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"country\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"US\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"US child SPM poverty rate, 2027\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027, first print in the Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"unit\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"percent\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"pointEstimate\\\\\\\":14.2,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":11.5,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":17.4,\\\\\\\"confidence\\\\\\\":0.8,\\\\\\\"resolutionDate\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2028-09-12\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionSource\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release and CPS ASEC Supplemental Poverty Measure table B-2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionRule\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Resolve to the first U.S. Census Bureau published calendar-year 2027 Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18, reported in percent, usually table B-2 of the annual Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release. Use the first print rounded to one decimal place; ignore later CPS ASEC, SPM-methodology, or table revisions unless the original release is formally corrected before the same release day ends.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"dataPointId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"census.cps_asec.spm.child_under18_poverty_rate.2027.first_print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"historicalContext\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2024 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":13.8},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2023 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":13.7},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2022 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":12.4},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2021 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":5.2},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2019 pre-pandemic child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":12.6}],\\\\\\\"drivers\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, medical expenses, work expenses, and geographic housing-cost adjustments\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"recent post-pandemic child SPM rates have re-centered near 13 to 14 percent after the temporary 2021 child tax credit expansion expired\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"first-print CPS ASEC sampling and processing noise warrants a wide interval even without a structural policy shift\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"sourceContext\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2025/demo/p60-287.html\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-283.html\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"runAt\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-27T14:15:49Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"reasoning\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Forecast for calendar-year 2027 child Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked Census annual income, poverty, and health insurance release pages and CPS ASEC SPM table B-2 for the recent child SPM reference class.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched child under-18 SPM poverty rates: 2024 = 13.8 percent, 2023 = 13.7 percent, 2022 = 12.4 percent, and 2021 = 5.2 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked the 2025 Census Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release page and linked SPM table for the latest first-print benchmark.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched latest first-print calendar-year 2024 child SPM poverty rate = 13.8 percent; all-person SPM poverty rate in the same release = 12.9 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked Census historical SPM table/report context for pre-pandemic and pandemic-policy anchor points.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched pre-pandemic 2019 child SPM poverty rate = 12.6 percent and temporary-credit 2021 child SPM poverty rate = 5.2 percent, showing a 7.4 percentage-point policy-sensitive drop versus 2019.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked the Census newsroom release calendar/annual income-poverty release schedule for the first-print release timing.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Calendar check used the Census release calendar and annual September income-poverty release pattern; the target first-print resolution date recorded for this run is 2028-09-12, with prior annual September first-print releases including 2025-09-09 for calendar-year 2024 data and 2024-09-10 for calendar-year 2023 data.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: absent a new 2021-scale refundable-credit expansion, the most relevant outside-view level is the post-expiration 2022-2024 range of 12.4 to 13.8 percent, with more weight on 2023-2024 because SPM thresholds, rent, medical expenses, and benefit take-up had mostly normalized after the pandemic emergency period.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Level and momentum: the recent level is already slightly above the 2019 pre-pandemic rate of 12.6 percent, and the 2023 to 2024 movement was essentially flat at +0.1 percentage point. That argues against extrapolating a large trend increase from the 2021-to-2022 jump, because that jump mostly reflected policy expiration rather than ordinary poverty momentum.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate into the upper teens, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Point calculation: start from a weighted recent baseline of 13.7 percent using 2022-2024 values, add +0.3 for loss of residual pandemic-era supports and higher SPM expense pressure by 2027, add +0.2 for recession/policy asymmetry because downside poverty risks are larger than upside income surprises, giving 14.2 percent. Interval: realized first-print child SPM swings include 2021 to 2022 = +7.2 pp and 2022 to 2024 = +1.4 pp; excluding the extreme temporary-credit reversal, a normal 80 percent band around 14.2 is about +/-2.5 pp, widened and right-skewed for policy/labor risk to 11.5 to 17.4.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\":14.2,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":11.5,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":17.4}]}\\\"}}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/revision_prompt.md:10:- series: census.spm.child_poverty_rate.2027\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/revision_prompt.md:11:- period: calendar year 2027\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/revision_prompt.md:112:{\\\"slug\\\":\\\"census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027\\\",\\\"country\\\":\\\"US\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"data\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"US child SPM poverty rate, 2027\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027, first print in the Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release.\\\",\\\"unit\\\":\\\"percent\\\",\\\"pointEstimate\\\":14.2,\\\"ciLow\\\":11.5,\\\"ciHigh\\\":17.4,\\\"confidence\\\":0.8,\\\"resolutionDate\\\":\\\"2028-09-12\\\",\\\"resolutionSource\\\":\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release and CPS ASEC Supplemental Poverty Measure table B-2\\\",\\\"reso