283/tableB-2.xlsx\\\",\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\"],\\\"runAt\\\":\\\"2026-06-27T14:15:49Z\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":[{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"heading\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Forecast for calendar-year 2027 child Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Census annual income, poverty, and health insurance release pages and CPS ASEC SPM table B-2 for the recent child SPM reference class.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched child under-18 SPM poverty rates: 2024 = 13.8 percent, 2023 = 13.7 percent, 2022 = 12.4 percent, and 2021 = 5.2 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked the 2025 Census Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release page and linked SPM table for the latest first-print benchmark.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched latest first-print calendar-year 2024 child SPM poverty rate = 13.8 percent; all-person SPM poverty rate in the same release = 12.9 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Census historical SPM table/report context for pre-pandemic and pandemic-policy anchor points.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched pre-pandemic 2019 child SPM poverty rate = 12.6 percent and temporary-credit 2021 child SPM poverty rate = 5.2 percent, showing a 7.4 percentage-point policy-sensitive drop versus 2019.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked the Census newsroom release calendar/annual income-poverty release schedule for the first-print release timing.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Calendar check used the Census release calendar and annual September income-poverty release pattern; the target first-print resolution date recorded for this run is 2028-09-12, with prior annual September first-print releases including 2025-09-09 for calendar-year 2024 data and 2024-09-10 for calendar-year 2023 data.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: absent a new 2021-scale refundable-credit expansion, the most relevant outside-view level is the post-expiration 2022-2024 range of 12.4 to 13.8 percent, with more weight on 2023-2024 because SPM thresholds, rent, medical expenses, and benefit take-up had mostly normalized after the pandemic emergency period.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Level and momentum: the recent level is already slightly above the 2019 pre-pandemic rate of 12.6 percent, and the 2023 to 2024 movement was essentially flat at +0.1 percentage point. That argues against extrapolating a large trend increase from the 2021-to-2022 jump, because that jump mostly reflected policy expiration rather than ordinary poverty momentum.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate into the upper teens, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Point calculation: start from a weighted recent baseline of 13.7 percent using 2022-2024 values, add +0.3 for loss of residual pandemic-era supports and higher SPM expense pressure by 2027, add +0.2 for recession/policy asymmetry because downside poverty risks are larger than upside income surprises, giving 14.2 percent. Interval: realized first-print child SPM swings include 2021 to 2022 = +7.2 pp and 2022 to 2024 = +1.4 pp; excluding the extreme temporary-credit reversal, a normal 80 percent band around 14.2 is about +/-2.5 pp, widened and right-skewed for policy/labor risk to 11.5 to 17.4.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"forecast\\\",\\\"point\\\":14.2,\\\"ciLow\\\":11.5,\\\"ciHigh\\\":17.4}]}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/revision_prompt.md:115:{\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Draft is mostly coherent, but the resolution date appears inferred from release patterns rather than verified from an official 2028 Census calendar or placeholder, which is blocking for publication.\\\",\\\"requiredFixes\\\":[{\\\"rubricItem\\\":\\\"resolver\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"blocking\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"resolutionDate is set to 2028-09-12, but the draft evidence only cites the Census release calendar and prior September release pattern, not an official 2028 first-print calendar entry or release placeholder.\\\",\\\"actionRequested\\\":\\\"Verify the 2027 SPM first-print release date from an official Census 2028 calendar, release page, schedule placeholder, or explicit policy rule; otherwise change the field/rule so it does not present an inferred date as verified.\\\"},{\\\"rubricItem\\\":\\\"interval\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"warning\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"The 80% interval is described as based on realized swings, but the conversion from historical volatility to 11.5-17.4 is only loosely specified and mixes excluded extreme-policy movement with discretionary widening.\\\",\\\"actionRequested\\\":\\\"State the realized-volatility basis more explicitly, such as which years are included/excluded and how that supports the lower and upper bounds, or label the interval as judgmental uncertainty.\\\"}],\\\"optionalSuggestions\\\":[\\\"Clarify whether table B-2 is the binding resolver if the annual release changes table numbering or format.\\\",\\\"Make the tail scenarios more numeric, for example naming approximate policy or labor-market conditions that would put the rate below 12% or above 17%.\\\"]}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t12-54-48z-statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04/stdout.txt:1:{\\\"slug\\\":\\\"statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04\\\",\\\"country\\\":\\\"CA\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"data\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Canada GDP by industry, Apr 2026\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"What will Statistics Canada first report as the month-over-month percent change in seasonally adjusted real gross domestic product by industry, all industries, at basic prices, for April 2026?\\\",\\\"unit\\\":\\\"percent_growth\\\",\\\"pointEstimate\\\":0.4,\\\"ciLow\\\":0.1,\\\"ciHigh\\\":0.7,\\\"confidence\\\":0.8,\\\"resolutionDate\\\":\\\"2026-06-30\\\",\\\"resolutionSource\\\":\\\"Statistics Canada Table 36-10-0434-01 and The Daily release for Gross domestic product by industry, April 2026\\\",\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\":\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/t1/tbl1/en/tv.action?pid=3610043401\\\",\\\"resolutionRule\\\":\\\"Resolve to the first Statistics Canada publication on June 30, 2026 for April 2026 seasonally adjusted real GDP by industry, all industries, month-to-month percent change, rounded to one decimal percentage point. If the June 30, 2026 Daily text and Table 36-10-0434-01 disagree at first publication, use the table value for all industries month-to-month percent change. Ignore later revisions.\\\",\\\"dataPointId\\\":\\\"statcan.36-10-0434-01.all_industries.month_to_month_percent_change.2026-04.first_print\\\",\\\"historicalContext\\\":[{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2026-03 first print\\\",\\\"value\\\":-0.1},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2026-02 first print\\\",\\\"value\\\":0.2},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2026-01 first print\\\",\\\"value\\\":0},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2026-04 advance estimate\\\",\\\"value\\\":0.4},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2025-12 first print\\\",\\\"value\\\":0.2}],\\\"drivers\\\":[\\\"Statistics Canada advance estimate points to 0.4 percent April growth\\\",\\\"Mining, oil and gas rebound after March contraction supports upside\\\",\\\"Manufacturing and transportation strength support goods momentum\\\",\\\"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting cited as an offset\\\",\\\"Recent monthly first prints cluster near 0.0 to 0.2 percent\\\"],\\\"sourceContext\\\":[\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/release-diffusion/2026-eng.pdf\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/t1/tbl1/en/tv.action?pid=3610043401\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260529/dq260529b-eng.htm\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260529/t001b-eng.htm\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260430/dq260430a-eng.htm\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260331/dq260331a-eng.htm\\\",\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260227/dq260227b-eng.htm\\\"],\\\"runAt\\\":\\\"2026-06-27T12:54:53Z\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":[{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"heading\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Forecast for Canada April 2026 real GDP by industry monthly growth\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Resolver is the first Statistics Canada print for seasonally adjusted real GDP by industry, all industries, month-to-month percent change for April 2026. The target is the June 30, 2026 Daily/table release, not later revised history.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Statistics Canada 2026-2027 major economic release dates PDF for Gross domestic product by industry.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"The schedule lists Gross domestic product by industry for April 2026 with release date June 30, 2026; adjacent GDP by industry releases are May 29, 2026 for March 2026 and July 31, 2026 for May 2026.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Fetched Statistics Canada The Daily release for Gross domestic product by industry, March 2026.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"The March 2026 release reported real GDP by industry at -0.1% month to month, after February +0.2%, and gave an April 2026 advance estimate of +0.4%; goods-producing industries were -0.8% in March and services-producing industries were +0.1%.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Fetched Statistics Canada table attached to the March 2026 Daily release.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Table 1 shows all industries month-to-month changes of -0.3 for October 2025, 0.1 for November 2025, 0.1 for December 2025, -0.0 for January 2026, 0.2 for February 2026, and -0.1 for March 2026; March 2026 annual-rate level was 2,339,731 million dollars.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Fetched prior Statistics Canada The Daily releases for January, February, and December context.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"January 2026 table context was -0.0%, February 2026 first text print was +0.2%, and December 2025 first text print was +0.2%; the February release's advance estimate for March was essentially unchanged, later first printed as -0.1%.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Base rate/reference class: recent official first prints around this series are small positives, with December +0.2, January -0.0, February +0.2, and March -0.1. The simple recent first-print average is about +0.1, but the agency's own April advance estimate is a strong near-term signal and usually deserves heavy weight.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Inside-view decomposition: level effects are modest because Q1 GDP by industry was only +0.1 for the quarter. Momentum is mixed after March weakness, but the named April advance components point to rebounds in mining, quarrying, oil and gas extraction, manufacturing, and transportation and warehousing. One-off factors include March maintenance/weather disruptions in energy and auto-related volatility that can reverse. Policy/trade mechanisms remain a drag risk through tariffs and manufacturing/export uncertainty.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration: the +0.4 advance estimate could be revised down if the March rebound in goods sectors was overstated, if agriculture/forestry/fishing weakness is larger than early information suggested, or if services activity failed to keep pace; an outside downside scenario is a flat or negative first print near 0.0 or below.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"I anchor at the recent first-print base rate near +0.1, put most weight on StatCan's April advance estimate of +0.4, and choose 0.4 as the rounded point. No separate ARIMA or ETS model was fit; the model prior is a recent-first-print persistence average updated toward the official advance estimate. The 80% interval is for the one-decimal rounded first print. Recent advance-to-first-print misses in visible releases were about 0.0 to 0.1 percentage point, but sector volatility and tariff/energy uncertainty justify a wider 80% interval of 0.1 to 0.7. Upside outside the interval would require a broad mining/manufacturing surge above +0.7; downside outside the interval would require advance-estimate reversal to 0.0 or lower.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Review disposition: accepted the resolver URL fix by using the official Table 36-10-0434-01 endpoint until the June 30 Daily URL exists, accepted the first-publication tie-break by making the table control if text and table disagree, and reconciled January 2026 context to -0.0/0.0 throughout. Also accepted the optional clar