rst print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":13.8},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2023 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":13.7},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2022 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":12.4},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2021 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":5.2},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2019 pre-pandemic child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":12.6}],\\\\\\\"drivers\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, medical expenses, work expenses, and geographic housing-cost adjustments\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"recent post-pandemic child SPM rates have re-centered near 13 to 14 percent after the temporary 2021 child tax credit expansion expired\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"first-print CPS ASEC sampling and processing noise warrants a wide interval even without a structural policy shift\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"sourceContext\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2025/demo/p60-287.html\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-283.html\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"runAt\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-27T14:15:49Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"reasoning\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Forecast for calendar-year 2027 child Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked Census annual income, poverty, and health insurance release pages and CPS ASEC SPM table B-2 for the recent child SPM reference class.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched child under-18 SPM poverty rates: 2024 = 13.8 percent, 2023 = 13.7 percent, 2022 = 12.4 percent, and 2021 = 5.2 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked the 2025 Census Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release page and linked SPM table for the latest first-print benchmark.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched latest first-print calendar-year 2024 child SPM poverty rate = 13.8 percent; all-person SPM poverty rate in the same release = 12.9 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked Census historical SPM table/report context for pre-pandemic and pandemic-policy anchor points.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched pre-pandemic 2019 child SPM poverty rate = 12.6 percent and temporary-credit 2021 child SPM poverty rate = 5.2 percent, showing a 7.4 percentage-point policy-sensitive drop versus 2019.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked the Census newsroom release calendar/annual income-poverty release schedule for the first-print release timing.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Calendar check used the Census release calendar and annual September income-poverty release pattern; the target first-print resolution date recorded for this run is 2028-09-12, with prior annual September first-print releases including 2025-09-09 for calendar-year 2024 data and 2024-09-10 for calendar-year 2023 data.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: absent a new 2021-scale refundable-credit expansion, the most relevant outside-view level is the post-expiration 2022-2024 range of 12.4 to 13.8 percent, with more weight on 2023-2024 because SPM thresholds, rent, medical expenses, and benefit take-up had mostly normalized after the pandemic emergency period.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Level and momentum: the recent level is already slightly above the 2019 pre-pandemic rate of 12.6 percent, and the 2023 to 2024 movement was essentially flat at +0.1 percentage point. That argues against extrapolating a large trend increase from the 2021-to-2022 jump, because that jump mostly reflected policy expiration rather than ordinary poverty momentum.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate into the upper teens, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Point calculation: start from a weighted recent baseline of 13.7 percent using 2022-2024 values, add +0.3 for loss of residual pandemic-era supports and higher SPM expense pressure by 2027, add +0.2 for recession/policy asymmetry because downside poverty risks are larger than upside income surprises, giving 14.2 percent. Interval: realized first-print child SPM swings include 2021 to 2022 = +7.2 pp and 2022 to 2024 = +1.4 pp; excluding the extreme temporary-credit reversal, a normal 80 percent band around 14.2 is about +/-2.5 pp, widened and right-skewed for policy/labor risk to 11.5 to 17.4.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\":14.2,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":11.5,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":17.4}]}\\\"}}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/pre_submit_review_codex_stdout.jsonl:3:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_0\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Draft is mostly coherent, but the resolution date appears inferred from release patterns rather than verified from an official 2028 Census calendar or placeholder, which is blocking for publication.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"requiredFixes\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"rubricItem\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"resolver\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"resolutionDate is set to 2028-09-12, but the draft evidence only cites the Census release calendar and prior September release pattern, not an official 2028 first-print calendar entry or release placeholder.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"actionRequested\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Verify the 2027 SPM first-print release date from an official Census 2028 calendar, release page, schedule placeholder, or explicit policy rule; otherwise change the field/rule so it does not present an inferred date as verified.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"rubricItem\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"interval\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"warning\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The 80% interval is described as based on realized swings, but the conversion from historical volatility to 11.5-17.4 is only loosely specified and mixes excluded extreme-policy movement with discretionary widening.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"actionRequested\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"State the realized-volatility basis more explicitly, such as which years are included/excluded and how that supports the lower and upper bounds, or label the interval as judgmental uncertainty.\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\"optionalSuggestions\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"Clarify whether table B-2 is the binding resolver if the annual release changes table numbering or format.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Make the tail scenarios more numeric, for example naming approximate policy or labor-market conditions that would put the rate below 12% or above 17%.\\\\\\\"]}\\\"}}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t12-54-48z-statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04/parsed_cells.json:69:        \\\"call\\\": \\\"Checked Statistics Canada 2026-2027 major economic release dates PDF for Gross domestic product by industry.\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t12-54-48z-statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04/draft_codex_last_message.txt:1:{\\\"slug\\\":\\\"statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04\\\",\\\"country\\\":\\\"CA\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"data\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Canada GDP by industry, Apr 2026\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"What will Statistics Canada first report as the month-over-month percent change in seasonally adjusted real gross domestic product by industry, all industries, at basic prices, for April 2026?\\\",\\\"unit\\\":\\\"percent_growth\\\",\\\"pointEstimate\\\":0.4,\\\"ciLow\\\":0.1,\\\"ciHigh\\\":0.7,\\\"confidence\\\":0.8,\\\"resolutionDate\\\":\\\"2026-06-30\\\",\\\"resolutionSource\\\":\\\"Statistics Canada The Daily release and Table 36-10-0434-01\\\",\\