d real GDP by industry at -0.1% month to month, after February +0.2%, and gave an April 2026 advance estimate of +0.4%; goods-producing industries were -0.8% in March and services-producing industries were +0.1%.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Fetched Statistics Canada table attached to the March 2026 Daily release.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Table 1 shows all industries month-to-month changes of -0.3 for October 2025, 0.1 for November 2025, 0.1 for December 2025, -0.0 for January 2026, 0.2 for February 2026, and -0.1 for March 2026; March 2026 annual-rate level was 2,339,731 million dollars.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Fetched prior Statistics Canada The Daily releases for January, February, and December context.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"January 2026 first text print was +0.1%, February 2026 first text print was +0.2%, and December 2025 first text print was +0.2%; the February release's advance estimate for March was essentially unchanged, later first printed as -0.1%.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Base rate/reference class: recent official first prints around this series are small positives, with December +0.2, January +0.1, February +0.2, and March -0.1. The simple recent first-print average is about +0.1, but the agency's own April advance estimate is a strong near-term signal and usually deserves heavy weight.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Inside-view decomposition: level effects are modest because Q1 GDP by industry was only +0.1 for the quarter. Momentum is mixed after March weakness, but the named April advance components point to rebounds in mining, quarrying, oil and gas extraction, manufacturing, and transportation and warehousing. One-off factors include March maintenance/weather disruptions in energy and auto-related volatility that can reverse. Policy/trade mechanisms remain a drag risk through tariffs and manufacturing/export uncertainty.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration: the +0.4 advance estimate could be revised down if the March rebound in goods sectors was overstated, if agriculture/forestry/fishing weakness is larger than early information suggested, or if services activity failed to keep pace; an outside downside scenario is a flat or negative first print near 0.0 or below.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"I anchor at the recent first-print base rate near +0.1, put most weight on StatCan's April advance estimate of +0.4, and choose 0.4 as the rounded point. Recent advance-to-first-print misses in visible releases were about 0.0 to 0.1 percentage point, but sector volatility and tariff/energy uncertainty justify a wider 80% interval of 0.1 to 0.7. Upside outside the interval would require a broad mining/manufacturing surge above +0.7; downside outside the interval would require advance-estimate reversal to 0.0 or lower.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"forecast\\\",\\\"point\\\":0.4,\\\"ciLow\\\":0.1,\\\"ciHigh\\\":0.7}]}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/validation.json:5:      \\\"slug\\\": \\\"census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/pre_submit_review_codex_events.jsonl:3:{\\\"type\\\": \\\"item.completed\\\", \\\"item\\\": {\\\"id\\\": \\\"item_0\\\", \\\"type\\\": \\\"agent_message\\\", \\\"text\\\": \\\"{\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Draft is mostly coherent, but the resolution date appears inferred from release patterns rather than verified from an official 2028 Census calendar or placeholder, which is blocking for publication.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"requiredFixes\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"rubricItem\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"resolver\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"blocking\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"resolutionDate is set to 2028-0