ed_cells.json\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/cells.with_activity.json:332:        \\\"path\\\": \\\"records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/validation.json\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/cells.with_activity.json:348:      \\\"draftArtifactPath\\\": \\\"records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/draft_stdout.txt\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/cells.with_activity.json:349:      \\\"reviewArtifactPath\\\": \\\"records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/pre_submit_review_stdout.txt\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/cells.with_activity.json:350:      \\\"revisionPromptPath\\\": \\\"records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/revision_prompt.md\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/cells.with_activity.json:357:          \\\"actionRequested\\\": \\\"Verify the 2027 SPM first-print release date from an official Census 2028 calendar, release page, schedule placeholder, or explicit policy rule; otherwise change the field/rule so it does not present an inferred date as verified.\\\"\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t12-54-48z-statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04/codex_stdout.jsonl:3:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_0\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-04\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"country\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CA\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Canada GDP by industry, Apr 2026\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"What will Statistics Canada first report as the month-over-month percent change in seasonally adjusted real gross domestic product by industry, all industries, at basic prices, for April 2026?\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"unit\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"percent_growth\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"pointEstimate\\\\\\\":0.4,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":0.1,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":0.7,\\\\\\\"confidence\\\\\\\":0.8,\\\\\\\"resolutionDate\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-30\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionSource\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Statistics Canada Table 36-10-0434-01 and The Daily release for Gross domestic product by industry, April 2026\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/t1/tbl1/en/tv.action?pid=3610043401\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionRule\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Resolve to the first Statistics Canada publication on June 30, 2026 for April 2026 seasonally adjusted real GDP by industry, all industries, month-to-month percent change, rounded to one decimal percentage point. If the June 30, 2026 Daily text and Table 36-10-0434-01 disagree at first publication, use the table value for all industries month-to-month percent change. Ignore later revisions.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"dataPointId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"statcan.36-10-0434-01.all_industries.month_to_month_percent_change.2026-04.first_print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"historicalContext\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-03 first print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":-0.1},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-02 first print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":0.2},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-01 first print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":0},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-04 advance estimate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":0.4},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2025-12 first print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":0.2}],\\\\\\\"drivers\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"Statistics Canada advance estimate points to 0.4 percent April growth\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Mining, oil and gas rebound after March contraction supports upside\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Manufacturing and transportation strength support goods momentum\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Agriculture, forestry, fishing and hunting cited as an offset\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Recent monthly first prints cluster near 0.0 to 0.2 percent\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"sourceContext\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/release-diffusion/2026-eng.pdf\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/t1/tbl1/en/tv.action?pid=3610043401\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260529/dq260529b-eng.htm\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260529/t001b-eng.htm\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260430/dq260430a-eng.htm\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260331/dq260331a-eng.htm\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/260227/dq260227b-eng.htm\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"runAt\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-27T12:54:53Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"reasoning\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Forecast for Canada April 2026 real GDP by industry monthly growth\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Resolver is the first Statistics Canada print for seasonally adjusted real GDP by industry, all industries, month-to-month percent change for April 2026. The target is the June 30, 2026 Daily/table release, not later revised history.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked Statistics Canada 2026-2027 major economic release dates PDF for Gross domestic product by industry.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The schedule lists Gross domestic product by industry for April 2026 with release date June 30, 2026; adjacent GDP by industry releases are May 29, 2026 for March 2026 and July 31, 2026 for May 2026.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched Statistics Canada The Daily release for Gross domestic product by industry, March 2026.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The March 2026 release reported real GDP by industry at -0.1% month to month, after February +0.2%, and gave an April 2026 advance estimate of +0.4%; goods-producing industries were -0.8% in March and services-producing industries were +0.1%.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched Statistics Canada table attached to the March 2026 Daily release.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Table 1 shows all industries month-to-month changes of -0.3 for October 2025, 0.1 for November 2025, 0.1 for December 2025, -0.0 for January 2026, 0.2 for February 2026, and -0.1 for March 2026; March 2026 annual-rate level was 2,339,731 million dollars.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched prior Statistics Canada The Daily releases for January, February, and December context.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"January 2026 table context was -0.0%, February 2026 first text print was +0.2%, and December 2025 first text print was +0.2%; the February release's advance estimate for March was essentially unchanged, later first printed as -0.1%.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Base rate/reference class: recent official first prints around this series are small positives, with December +0.2, January -0.0, February +0.2, and March -0.1. The simple recent first-print average is about +0.1, but the agency's own April advance estimate is a strong near-term signal and usually deserves heavy weight.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Inside-view decomposition: level effects are modest because Q1 GDP by industry was only +0.1 for the quarter. Momentum is mixed after March weakness, but the named April advance components point to rebounds in mining, quarrying, oil and gas extraction, manufacturing, and transportation and warehousing. One-off factors include March maintenance/weather disruptions in energy and auto-related volatility that can reverse. Policy/trade mechanisms remain a drag risk through tariffs and manufacturing/export uncertainty.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: the +0.4 advance estimate could be revised down if the March rebound in goods sectors was overstated, if agriculture/forestry/fishing weakness is larger than early information suggested, or if services activity failed to keep pace; an outside downside scenario is a flat or negative first print near 0.0 or below.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"I anchor at the recent first-print base rate near +0.1, put most weight on StatCan's April advance estimate of +0.4, and choose 0.4 as the rounded point. No separate ARIMA or ETS model was fit; the model prior is a recent-first-print persistence average updated toward the official advance estimate. The 80% interval is for the one-decimal rounded first print. Recent advance-to-first-print misses in visible releases were about 0.0 to 0.1 percentage point, but sector volatility and tariff/energy uncertainty justify a wider 80% interval of 0.1 to 0.7. Upside outside the interval would require a broad mining/manufacturing surge above +0.7; downside outside the interval would require advance-estimate reversal to 0.0 or lower.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Review disposition: accepted the resolver URL fix by using the official Table 36-10-0434-01 endpoint until the June 30 Daily URL exists, accepted the first-publication tie-break by making the table control if text and table disagree, and reconciled January 2026 context to -0.0/0.0 throughout. Also accepted the optional clarification that this is a rounded first-print interval and not a separately fit time-series model.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\":0.4,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":0.1,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":0.7}]}\\\"}}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/draft_stdout.txt:1:{\\\"slug\\\":\\\"census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027\\\",\\\"country\\\":\\\"US\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"data\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"US child SPM poverty rate, 2027\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027, first print in the Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release.\\\",\\\"unit\\\":\\\"percent\\\",\\\"pointEstimate\\\":14.2,\\\"ciLow\\\":11.5,\\\"ciHigh\\\":17.4,\\\"confidence\\\":0.8,\\\"resolutionDate\\\":\\\"2028-09-12\\\",\\\"resolutionSource\\\":\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release and CPS ASEC Supplemental Poverty Measure table B-2\\\",\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\":\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\",\\\"resolutionRule\\\":\\\"Resolve to the first U.S. Census Bureau published calendar-year 2027 Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18, reported in percent, usually table B-2 of the annual Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release. Use the first print rounded to one decimal place; ignore later CPS ASEC, SPM-methodology, or table revisions unless the original release is formally corrected before the same release day ends.\\\",\\\"dataPointId\\\":\\\"census.cps_asec.spm.child_under18_poverty_rate.2027.first_print\\\",\\\"historicalContext\\\":[{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2024 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":13.8},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2023 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":13.7},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2022 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":12.4},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2021 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":5.2},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"2019 pre-pandemic child SPM poverty rate\\\",\\\"value\\\":12.6}],\\\"drivers\\\":[\\\"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, medical expenses, work expenses, and geographic housing-cost adjustments\\\",\\\"recent post-pandemic child SPM rates have re-centered near 13 to 14 percent after the temporary 2021 child tax credit expansion expired\\\",\\\"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\\\",\\\"first-print CPS ASEC sampling and processing noise warrants a wide interval even without a structural policy shift\\\"],\\\"sourceContext\\\":[\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2025/demo/p60-287.html\\\",\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\\\",\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-283.html\\\",\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\\\",\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\"],\\\"runAt\\\":\\\"2026-06-27T14:15:49Z\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":[{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"heading\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Forecast for calendar-year 2027 child Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Census annual income, poverty, and health insurance release pages and CPS ASEC SPM table B-2 for the recent child SPM reference class.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched child under-18 SPM poverty rates: 2024 = 13.8 percent, 2023 = 13.7 percent, 2022 = 12.4 percent, and 2021 = 5.2 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked the 2025 Census Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release page and linked SPM table for the latest first-print benchmark.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched latest first-print calendar-year 2024 child SPM poverty rate = 13.8 percent; all-person SPM poverty rate in the same release = 12.9 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked Census historical SPM table/report context for pre-pandemic and pandemic-policy anchor points.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched pre-pandemic 2019 child SPM poverty rate = 12.6 percent and temporary-credit 2021 child SPM poverty rate = 5.2 percent, showing a 7.4 percentage-point policy-sensitive drop versus 2019.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Checked the Census newsroom release calendar/annual income-poverty release schedule for the first-print release timing.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Calendar check used the Census release calendar and annual September income-poverty release pattern; the target first-print resolution date recorded for this run is 2028-09-12, with prior annual September first-print releases including 2025-09-09 for calendar-year 2024 data and 2024-09-10 for calendar-year 2023 data.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: absent a new 2021-scale refundable-credit expansion, the most relevant outside-view level is the post-expiration 2022-2024 range of 12.4 to 13.8 percent, with more weight on 2023-2024 because SPM thresholds, rent, medical expenses, and benefit take-up had mostly normalized after the pandemic emergency period.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Level and momentum: the recent level is already slightly above the 2019 pre-pandemic rate of 12.6 percent, and the 2023 to 2024 movement was essentially flat at +0.1 percentage point. That argues against extrapolating a large trend increase from the 2021-to-2022 jump, because that jump mostly reflected policy expiration rather than ordinary poverty momentum.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate into the upper teens, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Point calculation: start from a weighted recent baseline of 13.7 percent using 2022-2024 values, add +0.3 for loss of residual pandemic-era supports and higher SPM expense pressure by 2027, add +0.2 for recession/policy asymmetry because downside poverty risks are larger than upside income surprises, giving 14.2 percent. Interval: realized first-print child SPM swings include 2021 to 2022 = +7.2 pp and 2022 to 2024 = +1.4 pp; excluding the extreme temporary-credit reversal, a normal 80 percent band around 14.2 is about +/-2.5 pp, widened and right-skewed for policy/labor risk to 11.5 to 17.4.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"forecast\\\",\\\"point\\\":14.2,\\\"ciLow\\\":11.5,\\\"ciHigh\\\":17.4}]}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/codex_stdout.jsonl:5:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"web_search\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"ws_0c12c3d9267fab78016a3fdb967ab08191b7f263d53d88b722\\\",\\\"query\\\":\\\"site:census.gov newsroom releases calendar 2028 Income Poverty Health Insurance Coverage United States 2027 Census September 2028\\\",\\\"action\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"search\\\",\\\"query\\\":\\\"site:census.gov newsroom releases calendar 2028 Income Poverty Health Insurance Coverage United States 2027 Census September 2028\\\",\\\"queries\\\":[\\\"site:census.gov newsroom releases calendar 2028 Income Poverty Health Insurance Coverage United States 2027 Census September 2028\\\",\\\"site:census.gov Income Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage United States 2027 release September 2028 Census\\\",\\\"Census release calendar 2028 income poverty health insurance 2027\\\"]}}}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/codex_stdout.jsonl:22:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_10\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"country\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"US\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"US child SPM poverty rate, 2027\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027, first print in the Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"unit\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"percent\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"pointEstimate\\\\\\\":14.2,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":11.5,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":17.4,\\\\\\\"confidence\\\\\\\":0.8,\\\\\\\"resolutionDate\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2028-09-30\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionSource\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"U.S. Census Bureau Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage in the United States release and CPS ASEC Supplemental Poverty Measure age table\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"resolutionRule\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Resolve to the first U.S. Census Bureau published calendar-year 2027 Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate for people under age 18, reported in percent in the annual Income, Poverty and Health Insurance Coverage release. Use Table B-2 if that table remains the SPM age, race, and Hispanic-origin table; if table numbering or format changes, use the equivalent first-print Census SPM table for age group under 18. Use the first print rounded to one decimal place; ignore later CPS ASEC, SPM-methodology, or table revisions unless the original release is formally corrected before the same release day ends. The 2028 Census release calendar entry was not yet available at this run, so this date is an operational end-of-September resolution deadline, with the value resolved on the actual first official print when posted.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"dataPointId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"census.cps_asec.spm.child_under18_poverty_rate.2027.first_print\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"historicalContext\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2024 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":13.8},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2023 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":13.7},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2022 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":12.4},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2021 first print child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":5.2},{\\\\\\\"label\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2019 pre-pandemic child SPM poverty rate\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\":12.6}],\\\\\\\"drivers\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"SPM includes taxes, refundable credits, noncash transfers, medical expenses, work expenses, and geographic housing-cost adjustments\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"recent post-pandemic child SPM rates have re-centered near 13 to 14 percent after the temporary 2021 child tax credit expansion expired\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"calendar-year 2027 rate is sensitive to refundable tax-credit law, SNAP and school-meal policy, child-care and medical out-of-pocket costs, and labor-market income\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"first-print CPS ASEC sampling and processing noise warrants a wide interval even without a structural policy shift\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"sourceContext\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2025/demo/p60-287.html\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2024/demo/p60-283.html\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/releases/calendar.html\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\"runAt\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-27T14:18:09Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"reasoning\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"heading\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Forecast for calendar-year 2027 child Supplemental Poverty Measure poverty rate\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The resolver is the Census Bureau's first published SPM poverty rate for people under age 18 for calendar year 2027. This is not the official poverty measure: SPM resources add taxes, refundable credits, and noncash transfers, and subtract necessary expenses including medical and work expenses, with thresholds adjusted for housing tenure and geography.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked Census annual income, poverty, and health insurance release pages and CPS ASEC SPM table B-2 for the recent child SPM reference class.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched child under-18 SPM poverty rates: 2024 = 13.8 percent, 2023 = 13.7 percent, 2022 = 12.4 percent, and 2021 = 5.2 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked the 2025 Census Poverty in the United States: 2024 release page and linked SPM table for the latest first-print benchmark.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched latest first-print calendar-year 2024 child SPM poverty rate = 13.8 percent; all-person SPM poverty rate in the same release = 12.9 percent; official poverty rate = 10.6 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked Census historical SPM table/report context for pre-pandemic and pandemic-policy anchor points.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched pre-pandemic 2019 child SPM poverty rate = 12.6 percent and temporary-credit 2021 child SPM poverty rate = 5.2 percent, a 7.4 percentage-point gap versus 2019.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"official.lookup\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Checked Census publication pages and newsroom calendar context for first-print timing and resolver availability.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Fetched annual first-print publication dates of 2025-09-09 for calendar-year 2024 data and 2024-09-10 for calendar-year 2023 data; no official 2028 calendar entry for calendar-year 2027 poverty data was available in the checked Census calendar at run time.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: absent a new 2021-scale refundable-credit expansion, the most relevant outside-view level is the post-expiration 2022-2024 range of 12.4 to 13.8 percent, with more weight on 2023-2024 because SPM thresholds, rent, medical expenses, and benefit take-up had mostly normalized after the pandemic emergency period.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Level and momentum: the recent level is already slightly above the 2019 pre-pandemic rate of 12.6 percent, and the 2023 to 2024 movement was essentially flat at +0.1 percentage point. That argues against extrapolating a large trend increase from the 2021-to-2022 jump, because that jump mostly reflected policy expiration rather than ordinary poverty momentum.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Policy mechanism: the child SPM rate is unusually exposed to refundable tax credits, SNAP, school meals, housing subsidies, medical expenses, and child-care/work-expense deductions. For 2027, my central case assumes no broad permanent 2021-style child allowance is in force for the full year, so taxes and transfers keep the rate near the post-pandemic plateau rather than returning to 2021's 5.2 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Counter-consideration: a material labor-market weakening by 2027 or benefit cuts could push the rate above 17 percent, while a large refundable child-credit expansion, unusually strong real wage growth for low-income families, or lower out-of-pocket medical and child-care costs could move it below 12 percent.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"math\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Point calculation: start from a weighted recent baseline of 13.7 percent using 2022-2024 values, add +0.3 for loss of residual pandemic-era supports and higher SPM expense pressure by 2027, add +0.2 for recession/policy asymmetry because downside poverty risks are larger than upside income surprises, giving 14.2 percent. Interval: using the non-2021-reversal post-expiration evidence, 2022 to 2023 was +1.3 pp and 2023 to 2024 was +0.1 pp, while 2019 to 2024 was +1.2 pp; I treat roughly +/-2.5 pp as normal 80 percent uncertainty around 14.2, then widen and right-skew judgmentally for tax-credit, benefit, and labor-market tail risk to 11.5 to 17.4.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Review disposition: accepted the resolution-date critique by no longer presenting 2028-09-12 as verified and by making the rule bind to the actual first Census print, with 2028-09-30 used only as an operational resolution deadline because the official 2028 calendar entry was not available. Accepted the interval critique by making the volatility basis and judgmental widening explicit; accepted the table-numbering and numeric-tail clarifications.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"kind\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"forecast\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"point\\\\\\\":14.2,\\\\\\\"ciLow\\\\\\\":11.5,\\\\\\\"ciHigh\\\\\\\":17.4}]}\\\"}}\\n\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"status\": \"completed\"}}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
records/thesis-analyst/2026-07-07/2026-07-07t15-49-35z-cms-medicaid-total-enrollment-2027-04/draft_codex_events.jsonl:37:{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "agent_message", "text": "{\"slug\":\"medicaid-enrollment-april-2027-work-req-deadline-holds\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"Medicaid and CHIP enrollment, Apr 2027\",\"question\":\"CMS national total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment for April 2027, preliminary Performance Indicator Data first monthly snapshot/data print, conditional on the 2025 reconciliation law Medicaid community-engagement compliance deadline taking effect on schedule with no federal statutory or regulatory delay announced by 2027-03-31.\",\"unit\":\"millions\",\"pointEstimate\":68.8,\"ciLow\":65.9,\"ciHigh\":71.7,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2027-09-30\",\"resolutionSource\":\"CMS Medicaid and CHIP Eligibility Operations and Enrollment Snapshot and Data.Medicaid.gov underlying monthly enrollment dataset\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://data.medicaid.gov/dataset/6165f45b-ca93-5bb5-9d06-db29c692a360\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolve to the first CMS-published national total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment count for the April 2027 reporting period in the preliminary Performance Indicator Data monthly snapshot or its underlying Data.Medicaid.gov dataset, converted from persons to millions and rounded to one decimal. Use the national total for the 50 states and DC, not a state row, weighted average, BHP count, T-MSIS separate CHIP table, or later revised/updated vintage; if CMS posts only preliminary monthly data before 2027-09-30, that first preliminary April 2027 national total governs. The condition is satisfied only if the community-engagement compliance deadline is in force on its statutory schedule and no federal statutory or regulatory delay has been announced by 2027-03-31.\",\"dataPointId\":\"cms.medicaid_chip.total_enrollment.2027_04.first_print.work_req_deadline_holds\",\"conditionalOn\":\"The 2025 reconciliation law's Medicaid community-engagement compliance deadline takes effect on its statutory schedule, with no federal statutory or regulatory delay announced by 2027-03-31.\",\"historicalContext\":[{\"label\":\"March 2026 total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment\",\"value\":74.294361},{\"label\":\"February 2026 total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment\",\"value\":74.9},{\"label\":\"January 2026 total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment\",\"value\":75.3},{\"label\":\"December 2025 total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment\",\"value\":75.7},{\"label\":\"October 2025 total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment\",\"value\":76.8}],\"drivers\":[\"post-unwinding enrollment drift still negative in early 2026\",\"January 1 2027 community-engagement requirement affects some adult Medicaid eligibility\",\"April 2027 is early enough that state implementation lag limits full work-requirement losses\",\"California March 2026 reporting revision lowers the latest national base\",\"first-print preliminary Performance Indicator Data may differ from later updated files\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid/program-information/medicaid-and-chip-enrollment-data/report-highlights\",\"https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid-and-chip-eligibility-operations-and-enrollment-snapshot\",\"https://data.medicaid.gov/dataset/6165f45b-ca93-5bb5-9d06-db29c692a360\",\"https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/downloads/eligib-oper-and-enrol-snap-mar2026.pdf\",\"https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/downloads/eligib-oper-and-enrol-snap-feb2026.pdf\",\"https://www.medicaid.gov/resources-for-states/working-families-tax-cut-legislation/community-engagement\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-07-07T15:49:43Z\",\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"Forecast for April 2027 Medicaid and CHIP enrollment if the work-requirement deadline holds\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Framing and exact resolver: this is the CMS preliminary Performance Indicator Data national total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment count for April 2027, converted to millions. The target is a national total for the 50 states and DC, not a weighted average, state row, BHP count, or T-MSIS separate CHIP variant. I use the fixed-vintage first monthly print and ignore later revisions.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the CMS March 2026 Medicaid and CHIP Enrollment Data Highlights page and linked Data.Medicaid.gov complete dataset for the latest national total.\",\"result\":\"Fetched March 2026 total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment = 74,294,361 people, with Medicaid enrollment = 67,080,865 and CHIP enrollment = 7,213,496; page says the March 2026 data were last updated June 26, 2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked CMS February 2026 and January 2026 Eligibility Operations and Enrollment Snapshot PDFs for recent monthly levels and renewal conditions.\",\"result\":\"Fetched February 2026 total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment = 74.9 million, January 2026 = 75.3 million, February 2026 month-over-month decrease = 390,000, and January 2026 month-over-month decrease = 460,000.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked CMS December, November, and October 2025 Eligibility Operations and Enrollment Snapshot PDFs for the recent reference-class history.\",\"result\":\"Fetched December 2025 total Medicaid and CHIP enrollment = 75.7 million, November 2025 = 76.0 million, October 2025 = 76.8 million, and December 2025 month-over-month decrease = 290,000.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the CMS monthly snapshot index for release timing and the historical April reporting-period example.\",\"result\":\"Fetched official monthly snapshot release dates: January 2026 released 2026-04-24, February 2026 released 2026-05-29, March 2026 released 2026-06-26, and April 2025 released 2025-07-25; the operational resolver deadline is 2027-09-30 for the April 2027 first monthly print if it has posted by then.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"Checked the CMS Community Engagement page for the conditional policy mechanism and statutory timing.\",\"result\":\"Fetched CMS statement that Section 71119 of Public Law 119-21 requires states, beginning January 1, 2027, to condition Medicaid eligibility for applicable individuals on community engagement unless a state implements sooner; the page also lists an interim final rule posted 06/01/2026.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base rate/reference class: the recent official-source reference class is the post-unwinding monthly CMS Performance Indicator Data series from October 2025 through March 2026. It fell from 76.8 million in October 2025 to 74.294 million in March 2026, a five-month drop of about 2.5 million, but the series is still transitioning and includes the March 2026 California reporting revision noted by CMS.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level, momentum, one-off, and policy effects: the latest level is 74.294 million. Pure recent momentum at about -0.4 to -0.6 million per month would mechanically point near 68.0 million by April 2027, but I damp that because unwinding attrition should slow. The one-off March California limited-benefit reporting revision lowers the base but should not be repeated every month. The community-engagement mechanism adds adult Medicaid losses after January 1, 2027, but April 2027 is an early reporting period, so state notices, exemptions, appeals, and system timing limit the full effect in the first print.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior = March 2026 level 74.294 with a smoothed trend from the October 2025-March 2026 historical sample; adjustment components are -3.7 million ordinary drift through April 2027, -1.4 million early community-engagement net disenrollment, and -0.4 million reporting/administrative noise, giving 74.294 - 3.7 - 1.4 - 0.4 = 68.8 million. Interval method uses successive monthly changes from rounded official levels: Oct-Nov -0.8, Nov-Dec -0.3, Dec-Jan -0.4, Jan-Feb -0.4, Feb-Mar -0.606 million, so sigma = 0.20 million and 1.28*sigma = 0.26 million for one-month noise; I widen far beyond that to +/-2.9 million because the target is 13 months ahead and conditioned on a new national eligibility-compliance regime, not a one-month continuation.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: upside risk is that implementation is administratively slow, many adults qualify for exemptions, and late-2026 Medicaid losses stabilize, which would land above the interval near 72 million or more. Downside risk is that several large expansion states enforce compliance aggressively from January 2027 while ordinary renewal churn continues, which would land below the interval near 65 million or less. Outside the interval would likely require either broad implementation failure/delay without triggering the condition, or a sharper-than-expected early disenrollment wave concentrated in large states.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Arithmetic: latest official level 74.294 million. Central ordinary drift from April 2026 through April 2027 is -3.7 million, early work-requirement/compliance effect is -1.4 million, and reporting/administrative adjustment is -0.4 million, so point = 74.294 - 3.7 - 1.4 - 0.4 = 68.794, rounded to 68.8 million. Recent successive-change sigma = 0.20 million and 1.28*sigma = 0.26 million; regime-adjusted 80% half-width = 2.9 million, so ciLow = 68.8 - 2.9 = 65.9 and ciHigh = 68.8 + 2.9 = 71.7.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":68.8,\"ciLow\":65.9,\"ciHigh\":71.7}]}"}}
